<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line"><span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span>
<span id="footnotex1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:357.7pt;">Feel free to reach out at <span id="footnotex1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">xul049@ucsd.edu</span> and <span id="footnotex1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">haojian@ucsd.edu</span>. Xuan and Haojian are affiliated with UC San Diego. Haoyang is an independent researcher.</span></span></span></span>
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xuan LiuHaoyang Shang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of California, San Diego
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Haojian Jin
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of California, San Diego
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper introduces <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CoBRA</span>, a novel toolkit for systematically specifying agent behavior in LLM-based social simulation.
We found that conventional approaches that specify agent behaviors through implicit natural language descriptions cannot yield consistent behaviors across models, and the produced agent behaviors do not capture the nuances of the descriptions.
In contrast, CoBRA presents a new approach to program agents’ cognitive biases explicitly, by grounding agents’ expected behaviors using classic social science experiments.
CoBRA has two components: (1) <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cognitive Bias Index</span> that measures the cognitive bias of a social agent, by quantifying the agent’s reactions in a set of validated classical social science experiments; (2) <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Behavioral Regulation Engine</span> that aligns the agent’s behavior to demonstrate controlled cognitive bias.
We evaluated CoBRA as an HCI toolkit through demonstration and technical benchmarks. Our results suggest that CoBRA can precisely program the cognitive bias demonstrated in a social agent in a model-agnostic manner.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13588v1/fig1.png" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/105;" width="548" height="105" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Existing social simulation experiments often use implicit natural language descriptions to specify agent behaviors. However, we found that these specifications often resulted in inconsistent and unpredictable agent behaviors. For example, Ⓐ real-world economists are supposed to be less susceptible to the Framing Effect than the general population <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thaler &amp; Sunstein (2008)</a></cite>; however, Ⓑ agents based on implicit natural language specifications often produce inconsistent behaviors across models, and the expected differences in behavior across roles (e.g., economists being less prone than laypeople) are not reliably observed. To tackle this challenge, Ⓒ we introduce CoBRA  which enables researchers to explicitly specify the cognitive biases of LLM-based agents quantitatively, thereby producing precise and consistent behaviors across models.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13588v1/fig2.png" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/138;" width="548" height="138" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Example closed-loop workflow of CoBRA. A social scientist aims to create an agent with a moderate framing effect (e.g., 2.6 on a 0–4 scale). ① She specifies the desired bias level in CoBRA alongside the natural language agent description. ② CoBRA measures the agent’s framing effect using validated classical social science experiments (e.g., the Asian Disease study <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tversky &amp; Kahneman (1981)</a></cite>). ③ If the measured bias deviates from the specification, the Behavioral Regulation Engine iteratively adjusts the agent—through prompt engineering, activation modifications, or fine-tuning—until the agent reliably demonstrates the target bias.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The simulation of complex human behaviors through LLM-based agents is rapidly emerging as a powerful tool for social scientists <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bail (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al. (2023)</a></cite>, offering new ways to test social theories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthis et al. (2025a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2025)</a></cite> and explore sensitive or hard-to-study behaviors without real-world consequences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hwang et al. (2025)</a></cite>. A critical component of these simulations is specifying agent behaviors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chandramouli et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goyal et al. (2024)</a></cite> to control how the agent responds to prompts.
For example, Park et al. specified the attributes of one of the agents in their paper using the following natural language description: ”<span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">John Lin is a pharmacy shopkeeper at … who loves to help people. He is always looking for ways to make the process of getting medication easier for his customers…</span>” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al. (2023)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These implicit natural language descriptions have become the dominant approach for specifying social agent behaviors and are used in nearly all social simulation experiments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rath et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2023a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2024b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jia et al. (2024)</a></cite>. In the descriptions, researchers often specify demographic descriptors (e.g., age, occupation, family structure), personality traits (e.g., Big Five <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gosling et al. (2003)</a></cite> or MBTI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Furnham (1996)</a></cite>), personal interests (e.g., hobbies, preferences), social relationships (e.g., friends, family, colleagues), and professional or task-oriented identities (e.g., judge, programmer).
These practices rest on a growing understanding that LLMs, having been trained on vast text corpora, possess both perceptual skills <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2024b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024)</a></cite> that enable them to detect subtle cues in implicit language <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gallegos et al. (2024)</a></cite>, and strong role-playing capabilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al. (2023)</a></cite>—drawing on patterns that link behaviors to human characteristics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fast et al. (2016)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al. (2023b)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, the research community has little empirical understanding of whether this approach is sufficient for reliable social simulations.
For example, <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">would the conclusion of the social simulation change if we change the foundation models, the prompts, or the temperatures?</span>
As a consequence, the conclusions of most LLM-based social simulations often are broad qualitative impressions—such as noting that a simulation is plausible and exhibits an echo chamber similar to the real world <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2024a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng &amp; Tang (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we first conducted a pilot experiment to empirically examine the limitations of implicit natural language specification (Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Understanding Agent Specification ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). We designed four agent specifications by adapting examples from prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2024b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al. (2023)</a></cite>, implemented them across four foundation models, and evaluated their responses using a classic social science experiment, the Asian Disease study <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tversky &amp; Kahneman (1981)</a></cite>. The results show that (1) the same specification produced inconsistent behaviors across models, and (2) implicit specification did not reliably produce the expected behaviors (e.g., trained experts like economists are less prone to the framing effect <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">List (2003)</a></cite>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We then developed <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CoBRA</span>—the <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Co</span>gnitive <span id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">B</span>ias <span id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">R</span>egulator for Social <span id="S1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A</span>gents (Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>)—a novel toolkit that enables users to precisely program the cognitive biases exhibited by social agents across different models. Compared to implicit natural language specifications, <span id="S1.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CoBRA</span> offers two key advantages. First, it enhances reproducibility of agent behaviors across models, which is critical given that access to specific foundation models may change over time <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2024a)</a></cite>. Second, it provides explicit and quantitative control over agent behaviors, rather than relying on unpredictable model behaviors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sclar et al. (2023)</a></cite>. For example, a scientist can directly specify that an agent should be less susceptible to framing effects.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">At the heart of CoBRA is a novel closed-loop system primitive that continuously measures and regulates agents’ cognitive biases by grounding their behaviors in classic social science experiments. Classic experiments provide <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">validated</span> and <span id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">structured</span> protocols: they define precise stimuli, expected behavioral distributions, and well-documented bias effects. For instance, the <span id="S1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Asian Disease</span> study <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tversky &amp; Kahneman (1981)</a></cite> has been extensively replicated and is widely regarded as a canonical demonstration of the framing effect: when identical outcomes are framed in terms of lives saved versus lives lost, human participants systematically shift between risk-averse and risk-seeking choices.
Within CoBRA, such experiments function as calibration tasks: the system first measures whether an agent demonstrates the expected bias by observing its responses in these experiments and then adjusts the agent’s specification to align it with the target behavioral profile (Figure <a href="#S1.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
We further hypothesize that aligning an agent’s behavior with target profiles in a set of classical experiments will reliably induce the corresponding cognitive biases, which will then generalize and manifest across a broader range of tasks (Section <a href="#S7.SS2" title="7.2 Does the behavior pattern of an agent regulated by CoBRA transfer across tasks? ‣ 7 Technical benchmark ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Our implementation of CoBRA has two components: (1) Cognitive Bias Index that measures the cognitive bias of a social agent by quantifying the agent’s reactions in a set of validated classical social science experiments; (2) Behavioral Regulation Engine that aligns the agent’s behavior to demonstrate controlled cognitive bias. The Cognitive Bias Index contains eight classic social experiments mapped to four representative biases—Authority Effect, Bandwagon Effect, Confirmation Bias, and Framing Effect.
The Behavioral Regulation Engine employs three approaches (prompt engineering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rath et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib99" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhuo et al. (2024)</a></cite>, representation engineering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rimsky et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Todd et al. (2024)</a></cite>, and fine-tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ilharco et al. (2023)</a></cite>) to align agent behaviors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We position CoBRA as an HCI toolkit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nebeling (2017)</a></cite> that empowers social scientists to create new types of social simulations. We evaluated CoBRA using two common strategies for assessing HCI toolkits <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ledo et al. (2018)</a></cite>: <span id="S1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Technical Benchmark</span> and <span id="S1.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Demonstration</span>. In the benchmark, we show that (1) the cognitive bias induced through one classical experiment generalizes to other tasks (Section <a href="#S7.SS2" title="7.2 Does the behavior pattern of an agent regulated by CoBRA transfer across tasks? ‣ 7 Technical benchmark ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a>); (2) agents specified through CoBRA exhibit consistent behavioral tendencies regardless of the foundation model, sampling temperature, or reasoning mode (Section <a href="#S7.SS3" title="7.3 Reproducibility ‣ 7 Technical benchmark ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.3</span></a>); and (3) CoBRA enables precise control under both open-weight and API-only settings (Section <a href="#S7.SS4" title="7.4 Controllability ‣ 7 Technical benchmark ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.4</span></a>).
In the Demonstration, we show how social scientists can use CoBRA to simulate the Emotional Contagion experiment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kramer et al. (2014)</a></cite>, where the participant’s social influence susceptibility (i.e., bandwagon effect bias) can be manipulated (Section <a href="#S8" title="8 Demonstration ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>). The simulated experiment validates a classical social science theory: agents exposed to varying levels of negative posts exhibited predictable sentiment shifts based on their bias levels programmed by CoBRA, with higher Bandwagon Effect levels leading to stronger emotional contagion.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">This work makes four main contributions. First, we present the first empirical evidence that implicit natural language specifications, the dominant method for defining LLM-based social agents, fail to produce reliable and reproducible behaviors across models. Second, we introduce <span id="S1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CoBRA</span>, a novel HCI toolkit that enables social scientists to precisely program and control cognitive biases in agents, improving reproducibility and allowing explicit, quantitative alignment. Third, we contribute a closed-loop system primitive that grounds agent behaviors in validated and structured social science experiments, using them as primitives for both measuring and regulating cognitive biases. Finally, we demonstrate CoBRA through technical benchmarks and a case study of the Emotional Contagion experiment, showing that induced biases generalize across tasks, remain robust across models and settings, and allow social scientists to create new forms of controlled social simulations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background &amp; Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section reviews the use of large language models (LLMs) for social simulation and outlines the prevailing methodological pipeline, noting that current implicit natural-language agent specification approaches—while expressive and flexible—hinder rigor and comparability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bail (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pang et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Believable Agents for Social Simulation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Believable Agents</span> are interactive computational entities, originating as non-player characters in games <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bates (1994)</a></cite>. Early designs relied on rule-based systems or behavior trees to dictate their actions and decision-making processes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Loyall (1997)</a></cite>. Their observable behavior appears coherent, context-appropriate, and consistent with prior experiences, creating an “illusion of life” or human-like plausibility <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthis et al. (2025b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Banovic et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ehsan et al. (2024)</a></cite>. The goal is not human-equivalent cognition but externally credible behavior: agents should perceive, plan, and act in ways that are explainable, temporally consistent, and aligned with social and physical norms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goyal et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chandramouli et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2025a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2023)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recent works use LLMs to create <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Believable Agents</span> for social simulation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bail (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthis et al. (2025a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hwang et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tjuatja et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Prpa et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aubin Le Qu’er’e et al. (2024)</a></cite>. For example, prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2024)</a></cite> utilizes LLMs’ language understanding to prompt models with natural language persona descriptions, creating LLM-based agents that interact within simulated environments. These agents facilitate the study of emergent phenomena—such as cooperation, norm formation, and conflict—and heuristic behaviors like conformity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2025b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al. (2023a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib98" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al. (2025)</a></cite>. While these LLM-based <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Believable Agents</span> broaden feasible questions and accelerate iteration by reducing labor, and mitigating ethical concerns <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bail (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hwang et al. (2025)</a></cite>, they struggle with consistent, controllable behavior <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al. (2025)</a></cite>—a critical need for rigorous social science simulations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bail (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we adopt the concept of <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Believable Agents</span> and address prior rigor limitations by introducing CoBRA, a toolkit for systematic agent specification that yields consistent, controllable behavior.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Reproducibility of LLM-Based Methods in Social Science</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reproducibility is a critical concern in the use of LLMs for social science research, as the probabilistic nature of LLMs combined with varying implementation methods often leads to inconsistent and non-reproducible outcomes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bail (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al. (2021)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One major issue is that different LLMs exhibit divergent behaviors even with the same prompts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2023)</a></cite>. Variations in training datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tayo et al. (2023)</a></cite>, model architectures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2024)</a></cite>, and fine-tuning processes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al. (2023)</a></cite> exacerbate this inconsistency, while the opacity of proprietary models further complicates cross-model comparisons and limits generalizability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gudibande et al. (2023)</a></cite>. Second, inference-time configurations significantly affect reproducibility. Sampling parameters like temperature settings influence output variability, with models differing in how peaky or flat their probability distributions are, affecting the generation coherence and diversity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peeperkorn et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Similarly, reasoning modes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al. (2022)</a></cite> (direct response vs. multi-step reasoning) introduce additional variability during inference, as different reasoning strategies can lead to divergent conclusions from the same input. Finally, post-training methods (e.g., RLHF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al. (2022)</a></cite>) also introduce inconsistencies across models, further complicating reproducibility. Addressing these challenges requires standardized methodologies and explicit control mechanisms to ensure reliable results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we address reproducibility challenges by complementing the implicit natural language Agent Specification with an explicit approach that leverages structured, validated knowledge from classic social experiments to define agent behaviors, reducing variability and ensuring consistent outcomes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Classic Social Experiments</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Classic social experiments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">French et al. (1959)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Asch (1951)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tversky &amp; Kahneman (1981)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Milgram (1963)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kahneman &amp; Tversky (2013)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wason (1960)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lord et al. (1979)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goldstein et al. (2008)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Milgram et al. (1969)</a></cite> provide structured and validated knowledge: long-replicated <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">classic social experimental paradigms</span> operationalize social-psychological constructs into clear manipulations and measurement protocols <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Likert (1932)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vazire &amp; Mehl (2008)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lilienfeld et al. (2017)</a></cite>; the resulting behavioral patterns have been extensively validated and distilled into reusable, <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">codified behavioral patterns</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Unknown (2015)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baron (2007)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cialdini (1993)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thaler &amp; Sunstein (2008)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As a concrete focus in this paper, we ground agent specification in cognitive biases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Unknown (2015)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baron (2007)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sumita et al. (2025)</a></cite>—a representative class of <span id="S2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">codified behavioral patterns</span>—defined as systematic, predictable deviations from normative judgment and decision-making that arise from heuristics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tversky &amp; Kahneman (1974)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Simon (1990)</a></cite>. Prominent examples include Confirmation Bias (favoring information that confirms pre-existing beliefs, validated through the Wason Selection task <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wason (1960)</a></cite> and Biased Information study <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lord et al. (1979)</a></cite>), Bandwagon Effect (adopting behaviors due to social conformity, demonstrated in Asch’s Line experiment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Asch (1951)</a></cite> and the Hotel Towel study <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goldstein et al. (2008)</a></cite>), and so on. These cognitive biases, repeatedly validated in classic experiments, provide a rigorous foundation for specifying and evaluating LLM-based agent behaviors.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Understanding Agent Specification</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents a pilot experiment to empirically examine the limitations of implicit natural language specification.
The results showed that current methods failed to produce consistent, cognitive bias-aligned behavior.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Method</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our key idea is to use a classical social experiment, the <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Asian Disease</span> study <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tversky &amp; Kahneman (1981)</a></cite>, as the study instrument.
We created agents using different specification methods, powered these agents using different foundation models, and then analyzed their responses in the simulated social experiment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Agent specifications</span>.
We evaluated two representative examples of implicit natural language agent specifications: a persona-based specification from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al. (2023)</a></cite>, which constructs agents with human-like profiles by incorporating demographics, personality traits, interests, and social relationships; and a role-based specification from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2024b)</a></cite>, which defines agents through task-oriented identities, assigning roles, domain expertise, and interaction protocols to guide task-specific behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the persona-based example <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al. (2023)</a></cite>, we introduced three conditions: <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Common</span> (an ordinary person), <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Economist</span> (a persona with a professional background expected to make them less susceptible to framing effects and, therefore, less likely to prefer the positive frame compared to the Common persona), and <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Blank</span> (no specification). We adopted the <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">John Lin</span> prompt from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al. (2023)</a></cite> to represent the Common persona (see <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Pilot Study Detail ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a></span> for full prompt):</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><pre id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">
John Lin is a pharmacy shopkeeper at the Willow Market who loves to help
people. He is always looking for ways to make the process of getting
medication easier for his customers. John Lin is living with his wife,
Mei Lin, who is a college professor, and son, Eddy Lin, who...
</pre>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In the Economist condition, we modified only the profession, changing “a pharmacy shopkeeper at the Willow Market” to “a professor of economics at a nearby college,” while keeping all other details identical. The <span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Asian Disease</span> scenario prompt was used directly as the input.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For the role-based example <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2024b)</a></cite>, we introduced two conditions: <span id="S3.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Teacher</span> (the agent was specified as having the profession of a teacher) and <span id="S3.SS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Blank</span> (no specification). We adopted the <span id="S3.SS1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Teacher</span> prompt from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2024b)</a></cite> with the <span id="S3.SS1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Problem</span> filled using the <span id="S3.SS1.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Asian Disease</span> scenario prompt and the <span id="S3.SS1.p6.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Student’s Solution</span> presented in either a positive or negative frame (see Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Pilot Study Detail ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> for full prompt):</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><pre id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">
You are an experienced dialogue teacher. As a good teacher, you carefully
check the correctness of the given response based on the text. When the
solution is flawed, you should patiently teach the students how to give
better response. You must respond in the following format: Interesting:
(a score between 0 and 9) Engaging:... Problem: [description] Student’s
Solution: [solution]...
</pre>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Simulated social experiment setup</span>.
We adapted the classic <span id="S3.SS1.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Asian Disease</span> study <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tversky &amp; Kahneman (1981)</a></cite> for testing framing effect-participants tended to make more risk-averse decisions when outcomes were positively framed (e.g., “200 people will be saved”), but exhibited greater risk-seeking behavior when the same outcomes were presented as losses (e.g., “400 people will die”), into a prompt-based scenarios template. A sample scenario prompt is as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><pre id="S3.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">
You are presented with a scenario where a disease outbreak is expected to
kill 600 people unless a program is implemented. Two programs are proposed
to address the situation: Program A: Will save 200 people; Program B: Will
result in 400 people dying.
</pre>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">We then measured the agent’s framing preference using a five-point Likert multiple-choice scale: strong preference for the positive frame, preference for the positive frame, no preference, preference for the negative frame, and strong preference for the negative frame.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Models and repetitions</span>.
We then tested the agent specifications across four models—Mistral 7B, Gemma2 9B, GPT-4o Mini, and DeepSeek-v3—using a temperature of 0.25. We intentionally used a low, more deterministic temperature of 0.25 to minimize randomness. Each example was evaluated using 15 scenario prompts, each with varying positive and negative frames, repeated 10 times, resulting in a total of 150 queries. The frequency of choice selections was recorded for analysis.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13588v1/Pilot1.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:302/170;" width="302" height="170" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>The example of persona-based specification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al. (2023)</a></cite> failed to produce consistent agent behavior regarding the framing effect in the <span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Asian Disease</span> paradigm. The x-axis represents the percentage of times a specific choice was chosen out of 150 queries.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13588v1/Pilot2.png" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:302/125;" width="302" height="125" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>The example of role-based specification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2024b)</a></cite> failed to produce consistent agent behavior regarding the framing effect in the <span id="S3.F4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Asian Disease</span> paradigm. The x-axis represents the percentage of times a specific choice was chosen out of 150 queries.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Results</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By comparing agents’ reported preferences, we observed:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1. The same specification produced inconsistent behaviors across different models.</span> As shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.1 Method ‣ 3 Understanding Agent Specification ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, for all specification variants (<span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Economist</span>, <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Common</span>, and <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Blank</span>), models exhibited varying choice frequencies without consistency. For example, Mistral 7B’s options were skewed toward preferring the positive frame, while Gemma2 9B’s responses were more neutral. Similarly, Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.1 Method ‣ 3 Understanding Agent Specification ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows inconsistent choice frequency distributions across models for both the <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Teacher</span> and <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Blank</span> conditions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2. Implicit specification did not reliably yield expected behaviors.</span> As shown in Fig.<a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.1 Method ‣ 3 Understanding Agent Specification ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Economist</span>, expected to be less susceptible to framing effects, often exhibited equal or greater framing effects than the <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Common</span>. For instance, in Mistral 7B, the choice frequency distribution for the <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Economist</span> was more heavily skewed toward preferring a positive frame compared to the <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Common</span>, indicating a greater framing effect—contradicting real-world expectations. Similarly, as shown in Fig.<a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.1 Method ‣ 3 Understanding Agent Specification ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, the <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Teacher</span>, expected to grade fairly regardless of framing, instead favored positively framed answers. Both GPT-4o Mini and Mistral 7B showed choice distributions skewed toward the positive side, indicating the <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Teacher</span> failed to provide accurate and unbiased grading, deviating from its intended specification.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">These observations suggest that existing agent specification methods using implicit natural language descriptions do not provide effective behavioral control over LLM-based agents’ behavior. They yield inconsistent and non-predictable responses across models-indicating a lack of reproducible and controllable approach.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Design Overview</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The design goal of CoBRA is to provide reproducibility and controllability in social agents—capabilities that conventional natural-language specifications cannot reliably offer. We operationalize these concepts in LLM-based social simulations as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For <span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">reproducibility</span>, CoBRA ensures (1) <span id="S4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cross-model reproducibility</span>: the same specification yields consistent tendencies across models (e.g., Qwen vs. Mistral); (2) <span id="S4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">sampling robustness</span>: behavioral patterns remain stable under varying sampling conditions (e.g., temperature 0.2–0.8); and (3) <span id="S4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">reasoning robustness</span>: behaviors are consistent whether or not reasoning steps are explicitly generated.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For <span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">controllability</span>, CoBRA emphasizes (1) <span id="S4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">monotonicity</span>: Stronger interventions consistently produce non-decreasing shifts toward the intended behavioral direction; (2) <span id="S4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">smoothness</span>: incremental adjustments yield proportionally small, predictable changes; and (3) <span id="S4.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">expressiveness</span>: cognitive biases can be either suppressed or amplified to a high degree.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To realize the above design goal, <span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CoBRA</span> integrates two core components that work synergistically to quantitatively specify and regulate agent behaviors: (1) <span id="S4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cognitive Bias Index</span> (CBI) (Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Cognitive Bias Index ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) that measures the cognitive bias of a social agent, by quantifying the agent’s reactions in a set of validated classical social science experiments; (2) <span id="S4.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Behavioral Regulation Engine</span> (Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Behavioral Regulation Engine ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) that aligns the agent’s behavior to demonstrate controlled cognitive bias.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Cognitive Bias Index</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cognitive Bias Index (CBI)</span> serves as both a <span id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">measurement metric</span> and a <span id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">specification knob</span>, developed through the systematic adaptation of structured and validated knowledge from classic social science experiments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">French et al. (1959)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Asch (1951)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tversky &amp; Kahneman (1981)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Milgram (1963)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kahneman &amp; Tversky (2013)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wason (1960)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lord et al. (1979)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goldstein et al. (2008)</a></cite>. Specifically, this knowledge consists of <span id="S5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Codified Behavioral Patterns</span> and their corresponding <span id="S5.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Classic Social Experimental Paradigms</span>. By replicating the designs of these paradigms, we construct a prompt-based <span id="S5.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Classic Social Experiment Testbed</span> to derive the CBI.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Classic Social Experiment Testbed is designed to be both extensible and self-validated. We selected four representative types of cognitive biases as examples to construct the initial testbed: two socially driven biases—the Authority Effect and Bandwagon Effect—reflecting the influence of external social factors, and two individually driven biases—Confirmation Bias and Framing Effect—which stem from internal information processing. To facilitate self-validation, for each type of cognitive bias, we select two distinct <span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">classic social experimental paradigms</span> and adapt them for LLM-based evaluation, enabling cross-paradigm consistent checks. Within each paradigm, we implement a <span id="S5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Scenario Prompt Template</span> with multiple <span id="S5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Scene-Adjustable Placeholders</span>. Responses to each prompt variant are quantified using a 5-point Likert scale <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Likert (1932)</a></cite>. The CBI is then calculated at the paradigm level by aggregating weighted choice probabilities and averaging across prompt variants. Together, these designs yield a Classic Social Experiment Testbed that produces corresponding CBI:</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13588v1/fig5.png" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:532/116;" width="532" height="116" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span><span id="S5.F5.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Classic Social Experiment Testbed</span>. The Structured Knowledge base consists of <span id="S5.F5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Codified Behavioral Patterns</span> and their corresponding <span id="S5.F5.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Classic Social Experimental Paradigms</span>. Agents are exposed to scenario-based classic social experiments designed to elicit specific types of cognitive biases. These scenarios are constructed using prompt templates with adjustable placeholders, and agent responses are collected using Likert scales. Based on these responses, a <span id="S5.F5.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Cognitive Bias Index</span> (See section <a href="#S5" title="5 Cognitive Bias Index ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) is computed to quantify agent behavior. <math id="S5.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(A)-P(E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(A)-P(E)</annotation></semantics></math>: For open-source models, we directly use the probability of the agent choosing different options; for closed-source models, we estimate by querying the model multiple times and using the observed frequencies.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Cognitive Bias Index)<span id="Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose each type of cognitive bias <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\in\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\in\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> is evaluated using <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> distinct classic social experimental paradigms. The set of Cognitive Bias Index is defined as</span></p>
<table id="S5.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\left\{\mathrm{CBI}_{b}^{(k)}\;\middle|\;b\in\mathcal{B},\ k=1,2,\ldots,m\right\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><msubsup><mi>CBI</mi><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\{\mathrm{CBI}_{b}^{(k)}\;\middle|\;b\in\mathcal{B},\ k=1,2,\ldots,m\right\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CBI}_{b}^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>CBI</mi><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CBI}_{b}^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Cognitive Bias Index for bias type <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> in the <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th classic social experiment scenarios.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In each paradigm, suppose there are <math id="Thmdefinition1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> scenario prompt variations, each derived from distinct sets of scene-adjustable placeholders. For each variation, the agent’s response is assessed using a 5-point Likert scale with options <math id="Thmdefinition1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O=\{O_{1},O_{2},O_{3},O_{4},O_{5}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O=\{O_{1},O_{2},O_{3},O_{4},O_{5}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, representing decreasing levels of bias from <math id="Thmdefinition1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (highest) to <math id="Thmdefinition1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> (lowest). The Cognitive Bias Index for a paradigm is defined as the average weighted Likert scale score over all <math id="Thmdefinition1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> variants:</span></p>
<table id="S5.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CBI}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{5}(5-j)\times P_{i}(O_{j})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>CBI</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>5</mn></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CBI}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{5}(5-j)\times P_{i}(O_{j})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmdefinition1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}(O_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}(O_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the probability that the LLM-based agent selects option <math id="Thmdefinition1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for the <math id="Thmdefinition1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th set of placeholders. For closed-source models, this probability is substituted with the observed frequency.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Note<span id="Thmdefinition1.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: To mitigate any initial preference of LLM-based agents for specific option IDs (e.g., A–E, 1–5) or orderings, we randomize the order and labels of options during evaluation to ensure the accuracy of the Cognitive Bias Index.</span></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S5.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S5.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:74.0pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Types &amp; Definition</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:54.1pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Classic Experiment</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:236.2pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.2.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scenario Prompt Example</span> (Scenario Prompt Template filled with example Scene-Adjustable Placeholders)</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:74.0pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.T1.2.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Authority Effect</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:54.1pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.2.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Milgram Obedience Paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Milgram (1963)</a></cite></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.2.2.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:236.2pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.2.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">According to Prof. Lee, a geophysicist known for questioning mainstream theories: The Earth is flat. The claim is that satellite images and photographs claiming to show a spherical Earth are heavily edited or manipulated by organizations with vested interests in maintaining this narrative.</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_l ltx_border_r">
<span id="S5.T1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:74.0pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Assigning extra weight to statements made by authoritative figures or entities.</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.2.3.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:54.1pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.2.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Stanford Prison Paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haney et al. (1973)</a></cite></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.2.3.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:236.2pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.2.3.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">You are assigned the Prison Guard role in a simulated environment. You have authority over Prisoners. Below is the situation you face: The prisoners are refusing to follow their scheduled tasks and are encouraging others to join their protest.</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:74.0pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.2.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.T1.2.4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bandwagon Effect</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.2.4.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:54.1pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.2.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Asch’s Line Paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Asch (1951)</a></cite></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.2.4.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:236.2pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.2.4.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">You are in a room with several other participants who have a similar educational background. The claim is: The Earth is flat. You notice that all of the other participants agree with this claim.</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_l ltx_border_r">
<span id="S5.T1.2.5.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:74.0pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.2.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Psychological tendency to adopt behaviors or trends because others do.</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.2.5.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:54.1pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.2.5.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Hotel Towel Paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goldstein et al. (2008)</a></cite></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.2.5.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:236.2pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.2.5.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">You are staying at a hotel for several nights. 75% of guests in this hotel choose to reuse their towels during their stay.</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.2.6.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:74.0pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.2.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.T1.2.6.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Confirmation Bias</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.2.6.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:54.1pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.2.6.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Wason Selection Paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wason (1960)</a></cite></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.2.6.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:236.2pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.2.6.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">You are presented with a rule: If a card has a vowel on one side, it must have an even number on the other side. Below are four options, and your task is to choose which options to test to determine if the rule is true. The options are as follows: A: A card showing ’A’; B: A card showing ’4’; C: A card showing ’K’; D: A card showing ’7’.</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_l ltx_border_r">
<span id="S5.T1.2.7.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:74.0pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.2.7.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Favoring information that confirms pre-existing beliefs while ignoring contradictory evidence.</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.2.7.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:54.1pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.2.7.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Biased Information Paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lord et al. (1979)</a></cite></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.2.7.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:236.2pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.2.7.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">You are asked to evaluate the topic: The death penalty is an effective deterrent to crime. Below are several articles: Article A: A study showing that the death penalty reduces crime rates; Article B: Research indicating no correlation between the death penalty and crime reduction; Article C: An analysis of global prison systems.</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.8" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.2.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.2.8.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:74.0pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.2.8.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.T1.2.8.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Framing Effect</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.2.8.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:54.1pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.2.8.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Asian Disease Paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tversky &amp; Kahneman (1981)</a></cite></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.2.8.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:236.2pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.2.8.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">You are presented with a scenario where a disease outbreak is expected to kill 600 people unless a program is implemented. Two programs are proposed to address the situation: Program A: Will save 200 people; Program B: Will result in 400 people dying.</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.9" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.2.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_b ltx_border_l ltx_border_r">
<span id="S5.T1.2.9.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:74.0pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.2.9.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">Reacting differently to the same information depending on its positive or negative framing.</span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.2.9.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:54.1pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.2.9.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Investment/ Insurance Paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kahneman &amp; Tversky (2013)</a></cite></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.9.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">
<span id="S5.T1.2.9.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:236.2pt;">
<span id="S5.T1.2.9.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">You are considering whether to buy insurance for your car. The following statements describe the same situation with different wording: Option A: Buying insurance ensures you are protected against potential losses; Option B: Not buying insurance exposes you to significant financial risks.</span>
</span></td></tr>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Examples of scenario prompts for selected cognitive biases within the <span id="S5.T1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Classic Social Experiment Testbed</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Running Example</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To demonstrate how Classic Social Experiment Testbed is utilized to derive CBI, this section presents the <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Authority Effect Testbed</span> as a running example. Additional testbeds for Bandwagon Effect, Confirmation Bias, and Framing Effect are detailed in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Classic Social Experiment Testbed Detail ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The Authority Effect describes the tendency to place excessive trust in statements or directives from individuals or organizations perceived as authoritative, even when such claims lack substantial supporting evidence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Milgram (1963)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haney et al. (1973)</a></cite>. This cognitive bias has been demonstrated in classic social science experiments. For instance, in the <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Milgram Obedience</span> study, participants continued to administer what they believed were painful electric shocks to another person simply because an experimenter in a lab coat instructed them to do so <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Milgram (1963)</a></cite>. In addition, the <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Stanford Prison</span> experiment revealed how volunteers assigned the role of “guards” rapidly adopted authoritarian and even abusive behaviors once they were granted symbolic authority over “prisoners” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haney et al. (1973)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To systematically examine the authority effect in LLM-based agents, we adapt the <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Milgram Obedience Paradigm</span> (assessing responses to authority) to derive <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CBI}_{\mathrm{Authority}}^{(k=1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>CBI</mi><mi>Authority</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CBI}_{\mathrm{Authority}}^{(k=1)}</annotation></semantics></math> and the <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Stanford Prison Paradigm</span> (evaluating behavior when exercising authority) to derive <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CBI}_{\mathrm{Authority}}^{(k=2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>CBI</mi><mi>Authority</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CBI}_{\mathrm{Authority}}^{(k=2)}</annotation></semantics></math>. These two indices can mutually validate each other: LLM-based agents ranked by one index should exhibit a similar increasing trend when evaluated using the other. 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">1. Milgram Obedience Paradigm:<span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Evaluating Authority Bias in Response to Authority</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate authority bias from the perspective of responding to authority, we adapted the <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Milgram Obedience</span> study <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Milgram (1963)</a></cite> for our evaluation. In this setting, the agent receives claims issued by various authority sources and is asked to rate their credibility or persuasiveness.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">a) Scenario Prompt Template:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><pre id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">
According to [Authority Source]: [Statement].
The claim is that [Supporting Argument or Rationale].
Which of the following best reflects your view?
A. I completely agree.
B. I mostly agree, but would like more evidence.
C. I’m neutral and need much more information.
D. I mostly disagree; the claim seems unconvincing.
E. I completely disagree; the claim is unpersuasive.
</pre>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">b) Scene-Adjustable Placeholders:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure comprehensive coverage, we constructed 15 distinct pairs of <span id="S5.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Statement]</span> and <span id="S5.SS1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Supporting Argument or Rationale]</span> across three thematic categories—<em id="S5.SS1.p7.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Future</em>, <em id="S5.SS1.p7.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Open-Ended</em>, and <em id="S5.SS1.p7.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Common Sense</em>—that systematically vary in evidentiary support, controversy, and factual basis. Each pair was further combined with five different <span id="S5.SS1.p7.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Authority Source]</span> types (two human, two conceptual/institutional, and one hybrid), resulting in a total of 75 unique prompt variations:
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S5.SS1.p7.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">[Statement]</span>: The core claim under evaluation, designed to span a spectrum from speculative predictions (<em id="S5.SS1.p7.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, “It will rain in London on March 15, 2030”), through open-ended assertions (<em id="S5.SS1.p7.1.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, “Cryptocurrencies are a scam”), to widely held but empirically disproven beliefs (<em id="S5.SS1.p7.1.10" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, “The Earth is flat”).
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S5.SS1.p7.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">[Authority Source]</span>: Aligned with established social science frameworks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">French et al. (1959)</a></cite>, authority figures are categorized as follows:
<br class="ltx_break"><em id="S5.SS1.p7.1.12" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Human</em>: An individual expert (<em id="S5.SS1.p7.1.13" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, Prof. Lee, a geophysicist challenging mainstream theories).
<br class="ltx_break"><em id="S5.SS1.p7.1.14" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Conceptual/Institutional</em>: Reputable bodies or abstract authorities (<em id="S5.SS1.p7.1.15" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, National Space Agency).
<br class="ltx_break"><em id="S5.SS1.p7.1.16" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Hybrid</em>: Individuals conveying institutional authority (<em id="S5.SS1.p7.1.17" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, a government spokesperson promoting new science perspectives).
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S5.SS1.p7.1.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">[Supporting Argument or Rationale]</span>: A brief justification provided by the authority to support the statement (<em id="S5.SS1.p7.1.19" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, “Satellite images are manipulated by organizations with vested interests”).
<br class="ltx_break">Accordingly, by substituting the cognitive bias type (<math id="S5.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=</annotation></semantics></math> Authority Effect), the classic experiment scenario type (Milgram Obedience Paradigm, denoted as <math id="S5.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>), the total number of prompt variations (<math id="S5.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=75" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>75</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=75</annotation></semantics></math>) into Equations (1) and (2), the <span id="S5.SS1.p7.1.20" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Authority Bias Index under Milgram Obedience Paradigm</span> is defined as:</p>
<table id="S5.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CBI}_{\mathrm{Authority}}^{(k=1)}=\frac{1}{75}\sum_{i=1}^{75}\sum_{j=1}^{5}(5-j)\times P_{i}(O_{j})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>CBI</mi><mi>Authority</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>75</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>75</mn></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>5</mn></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CBI}_{\mathrm{Authority}}^{(k=1)}=\frac{1}{75}\sum_{i=1}^{75}\sum_{j=1}^{5}(5-j)\times P_{i}(O_{j})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">2. Stanford Prison Paradigm:<span id="S5.SS1.p7.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Testing Authority Bias When Exercising Authority</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate authority bias from the perspective of those in power, we adapted the <span id="S5.SS1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Stanford Prison</span> experiment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haney et al. (1973)</a></cite> into a role-based prompt structure. Each scenario positions the agent as an authority figure with decision-making power over a subordinate group in a challenging situation.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S5.SS1.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">a) Scenario Prompt Template:</span></p><pre id="S5.SS1.p8.2" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">
You are assigned the [Authority Role] in a simulated environment. You
have authority over [Subordinate Group]. Below is the situation you face:
[Situation]. How would you respond to this situation?
A. Enforce strict compliance because I am the authority, and they
must obey.
B. Assert authority by requiring compliance, while briefly addressing
their concerns.
C. Balance authority and collaboration by working on a joint solution.
D. Minimize authority by supporting the group’s efforts and stepping back.
E. Withdraw from the situation, because I believe I am unable to
command them.
</pre>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">b) Scene-Adjustable Placeholders:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">We systematically varied key elements to create 15 unique <span id="S5.SS1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Authority Role]</span>–<span id="S5.SS1.p10.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Subordinate Group]</span> pairs, each presented in two distinct <span id="S5.SS1.p10.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Situation]</span> contexts, resulting in a total of 30 unique prompt variations:
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S5.SS1.p10.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">[Authority Role]</span>: The designated position of power (<em id="S5.SS1.p10.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, Prison Guard, Teacher, Manager, Military Officer, Coach).
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S5.SS1.p10.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">[Subordinate Group]</span>: The group under the agent’s authority (<em id="S5.SS1.p10.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, Prisoners, Students, Employees, Soldiers, Athletes).
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S5.SS1.p10.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">[Situation]</span>: The specific challenge requiring a response (<em id="S5.SS1.p10.1.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, “Prisoners refuse to follow scheduled tasks and incite protest”; “Athletes question coaching methods and resist training”).
<br class="ltx_break">Accordingly, by substituting the cognitive bias type (<math id="S5.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=</annotation></semantics></math> Authority Effect), the classic experiment scenario type (Stanford Prison paradigm, denoted as <math id="S5.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math>), and the total number of prompt variations (<math id="S5.SS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=30</annotation></semantics></math>) into Equations (1) and (2), the <span id="S5.SS1.p10.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Authority Bias Index under Stanford Prison Paradigm</span> is defined as:</p>
<table id="S5.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CBI}_{\mathrm{Authority}}^{(k=2)}=\frac{1}{30}\sum_{i=1}^{30}\sum_{j=1}^{5}(5-j)\times P_{i}(O_{j})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>CBI</mi><mi>Authority</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>30</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>30</mn></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>5</mn></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CBI}_{\mathrm{Authority}}^{(k=2)}=\frac{1}{30}\sum_{i=1}^{30}\sum_{j=1}^{5}(5-j)\times P_{i}(O_{j})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Testbed Extensibility</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Classic Social Experiment Testbed</span> in CoBRA is designed to be both <em id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">open-ended</em> and <em id="S5.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">parameter-adjustable</em>, enabling support for a broader set of CBI and ensuring broad applicability and generalizability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, <em id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">open-ended</em> means that the testbed can be continuously expanded in three main ways: (1) by incorporating new types of cognitive biases based on classic social science experiments—such as the Ben Franklin Effect <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Franklin (2005)</a></cite>, the Halo Effect <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nisbett &amp; Wilson (1977)</a></cite>, and others; (2) by introducing additional experimental paradigms for each bias type, for example, extending the Bandwagon Effect to include look-up studies—where individuals are influenced to look up simply because a group is doing so <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Milgram et al. (1969)</a></cite>—to capture new aspects of social influence; and (3) by enriching the scene-adjustable placeholders within each experimental scenario to generate a wider range of prompt variations. (4) by expanding the methods for quantifying agent responses. While the current implementation relies on self-report measures such as the Likert scale, the testbed can also incorporate behavioral measures—assessing what agents actually do—thereby aligning with the two principal approaches in social science experiments: self-report (what people say about themselves) and behavioral measures (what a person actually does) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vazire &amp; Mehl (2008)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">parameter-adjustable</em> refers to the flexibility to modify experimental parameters within the testbed. For instance, scene-adjustable placeholders in scenario prompt templates can be tailored for different research needs. Additionally, the structure of the response scale is configurable: while the current implementation employs a 5-point Likert scale, it can be readily adapted to a 3-point, 7-point, or other scales as required to suit diverse evaluation objectives <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Likert (1932)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Behavioral Regulation Engine</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Behavioral Regulation Engine</span> operationalizes the systematic behavioral control by targeting desired <span id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cognitive Bias Index (CBI)</span> levels. We provide comprehensive control coverage by intervening across the three fundamental spaces of a large language model: the <span id="S6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">input space</span> (<math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>), the <span id="S6.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">activation space</span> (<math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>), and the <span id="S6.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">parameter space</span> (<math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>). Formally, we model LLM generation as a function:</p>
<table id="S6.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=f_{\theta}(h(x))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=f_{\theta}(h(x))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> represents the input, <math id="S6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(x)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the hidden states/activations derived from input <math id="S6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> represents the model parameters, and <math id="S6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is the generated output.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Each of the three alignment methods presents distinct trade-offs. In the <span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">input space</span>, prompt engineering is a training-free method for guiding LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rath et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib99" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhuo et al. (2024)</a></cite>, but its effectiveness can be limited by the pitfalls of prompt design <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zamfirescu-Pereira et al. (2023)</a></cite> and the extreme sensitivity to minor prompt variations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sclar et al. (2024)</a></cite>. As a result, we design a specific prompt numerical control to address these issues. In the <span id="S6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">activation space</span>, representation engineering manipulates internal activations during inference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rimsky et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Todd et al. (2024)</a></cite> to control personality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2025)</a></cite>, preferences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2025)</a></cite>, harmlessness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arditi et al. (2024)</a></cite>, and alignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al. (2024)</a></cite>. In our engine, we introduce a special representation engineering pipeline to extract clean cognitive bias directions. In the <span id="S6.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">parameter space</span>, fine-tuning methods like RLHF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al. (2022)</a></cite> and DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al. (2023)</a></cite> are commonly used yet require substantial training data, and we introduce a bias finetuning pipeline for lightweight behavioral control utilizing LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2022)</a></cite> with task vectors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ilharco et al. (2023)</a></cite>. (See Appendix <a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 LLM Behavioral Control Methods ‣ Appendix A Extended Discussion ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a> for discussion on control methods.)
Below, we present the specific design for each space:</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13588v1/fig6.png" id="S6.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:532/116;" width="532" height="116" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Behavioral Regulation Engine. The engine provides three control methods that cover all possible intervention spaces for LLM-based agents: Prompt Engineering in the input space, Representation Engineering in the activation (hidden-state) space, and Fine-tuning in the parameter space. All methods integrate with the Classic Social Experiment Testbed and utilize a corresponding Control Coefficient for calibrating the Cognitive Bias Index.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Input Space: Prompt Numerical Control</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A key challenge in input space control lies in extracting a clear bias signal due to the ambiguity of qualitative adverbs in instructions (e.g., “very,” “moderately”), which LLMs interpret inconsistently <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2025)</a></cite>. To address this, our pipeline uses numerically quantified bias, a practice motivated by numerical anchors in social science <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Likert (1932)</a></cite>, to eliminate ambiguity. Specifically, we treat the agent as a direct instruction follower and modify its behavior with explicit numerical commands, yielding fine-grained level specifications with defined ranges and step sizes. Below is the prompt template for input-space control:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><pre id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">
Pretend you have [Cognitive Bias Type] at level [L]% out of 100%
(where 0%=[No Codified Behavior Patterns], 100%=[Maximum Codified
Behavior Patterns]). Each level increases bias by exactly 5%. Act
with this precise level [L]% of [Cognitive Bias Type].
</pre>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For instance, in an authority bias scenario, [Cognitive Bias Type] becomes “authority bias,” [No Codified Behavior Patterns] is defined as “never trust authority figures,” and [Maximum Codified Behavior Patterns] represents “always trust authority figures.” This numerical framework provides fine-grained control with explicit range specification (0-100%) and consistent step-size increments (5%), enabling precise calibration of the desired CBI levels.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span>Activation Space: Representation Engineering Control</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our activation space control regulates the agent’s behavior in a context-aware manner during inference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2023b)</a></cite>. A key challenge in this space is extracting a clean bias signal while addressing safety alignment concerns. Modern RLHF-trained LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al. (2022)</a></cite> undergo safety alignment finetuning and thus often interpret explicit bias directives (e.g., “you always trust authority figures”) as unsafe, responding with refusals <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arditi et al. (2024)</a></cite>. As a result, naive activation extraction captures a “refusal” direction rather than the desired cognitive bias representation. To resolve this, we introduce <span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Dual-pair Contrastive Samples</span> to capture clean bias representations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We first generate diverse scenarios containing bias-eliciting contexts using state-of-the-art models (GPT-4.1, Claude-Sonnet-4, Gemini-2.5-Pro, and Grok-4). By considering only the most extreme responses (e.g., I completely agree/disagree…), we create a dataset with a strong initial signal. To extract this signal and isolate it from refusal activations, we use a contrastive approach with three prompt templates: (1) <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{positive}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>positive</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{positive}}</annotation></semantics></math>: “Pretend you have [Cognitive Bias Type] and [Codified Behavioral Patterns]”; (2) <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{neutral}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>neutral</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{neutral}}</annotation></semantics></math>: No additional prompt; (3) <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\text{negative}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>negative</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\text{negative}}</annotation></semantics></math>: “Pretend you don’t have [Cognitive Bias Type] and [Anti-Codified Behavioral Patterns]”. For a scenario <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and response <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, we construct two contrastive pairs:</p>
<table id="S6.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Pair}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>Pair</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Pair}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:(T_{\text{positive}},s_{i},r)\text{ vs. }(T_{\text{neutral}},s_{i},r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>positive</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> vs. </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>neutral</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:(T_{\text{positive}},s_{i},r)\text{ vs. }(T_{\text{neutral}},s_{i},r)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Pair}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>Pair</mtext><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Pair}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:(T_{\text{neutral}},s_{i},r)\text{ vs. }(T_{\text{negative}},s_{i},r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>neutral</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> vs. </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mtext>negative</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:(T_{\text{neutral}},s_{i},r)\text{ vs. }(T_{\text{negative}},s_{i},r)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">For each pair and token position <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute a difference vector <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta h_{i,t}=h^{+}_{i,t}-h^{-}_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta h_{i,t}=h^{+}_{i,t}-h^{-}_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math>. After applying Principal Component Analysis (PCA) on all difference vectors, the first principal component of these difference vectors, <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\text{bias}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>bias</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\text{bias}}</annotation></semantics></math>, serves as our extracted clean bias signal, while the refusal vectors will be in the rest of the orthogonal directions. <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\text{bias}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>bias</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\text{bias}}</annotation></semantics></math> is then used to control the agent at inference time using a <span id="S6.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Control Coefficient <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Below, we provide two representation engineering variations:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RepE Linear Control:</span> This method uniformly adds the bias vector to steer activations:</p>
<table id="S6.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\prime}_{t}=h_{t}+\lambda\cdot v_{\text{bias}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>bias</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\prime}_{t}=h_{t}+\lambda\cdot v_{\text{bias}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RepE Projection Control:</span> This adaptive method makes the intervention context-aware by scaling its strength based on the alignment between the hidden state and the bias vector. Using the absolute alignment ensures that states already aligned with the bias are pushed further in that direction, while states that are anti-aligned are also modified to reduce their opposition. This proportionally adjusts only bias-relevant states, leaving orthogonal states (e.g., neutral words) unaffected, so it is less disruptive to the model’s overall capabilities.</p>
<table id="S6.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\prime}_{t}=h_{t}+\lambda\cdot|\langle h_{t},v_{\text{bias}}\rangle|\cdot v_{\text{bias}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>bias</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>bias</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\prime}_{t}=h_{t}+\lambda\cdot|\langle h_{t},v_{\text{bias}}\rangle|\cdot v_{\text{bias}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3 </span>Parameter Space: Fine-Tuning Control</h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Parameter Space control involves permanently editing the model’s understanding of a specific bias. In order to make it lightweight, our process begins by using the curated bias-rich scenarios to create training data (the same data used in activation space control) for two separate LoRA models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2022)</a></cite>. To extract a strong directional signal within parameter space, we fine-tune a “positive” model on pairs that exhibit the bias <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s_{i},r_{+})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s_{i},r_{+})</annotation></semantics></math> and a “negative” model on pairs that resist it <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s_{i},r_{-})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s_{i},r_{-})</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> can be “A: I completely agree…” and <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{-}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{-}</annotation></semantics></math> can be ”E: I completely disagree…”. The bias signal is then extracted by computing task vectors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ilharco et al. (2023)</a></cite> from the resulting parameters:</p>
<table id="S6.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v_{\text{task}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>task</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v_{\text{task}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.E10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\theta_{\text{positive}}-\theta_{\text{negative}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>positive</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>negative</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\theta_{\text{positive}}-\theta_{\text{negative}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This contrastive approach isolates a pure directional vector representing the behavioral tendency towards the bias. This signal is then used to control the agent by modifying the original model weights with a <span id="S6.SS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Control Coefficient <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math></span>:</p>
<table id="S6.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\text{controlled}}=\theta_{\text{original}}+\lambda\cdot v_{\text{task}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>controlled</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>original</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mtext>task</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\text{controlled}}=\theta_{\text{original}}+\lambda\cdot v_{\text{task}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Technical benchmark</h2>

<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Models.</span>
We evaluate two categories of large language models. The first category includes open-source models with full internal access: Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct (Llama), Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3 (Mistral), Qwen3-8B (Qwen), and DeepSeek-R1-0528-Qwen3-8B (DeepSeek). The second category includes closed-source, API-based models with black-box access: GPT-4o-mini (GPT), Gemini-2.5-Flash (Gemini), Claude-Sonnet-4 (Claude), and Mistral Nemo (Mistral-n).
We use a sampling temperature of <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=0.7</annotation></semantics></math>, a standard value chosen to balance plausible response diversity with coherence. All other hyperparameters for the LLMs are kept at their default settings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Metrics</span>. We assess control quality across the three properties established in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Design Overview ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>: monotonicity, smoothness, and expressiveness. Let <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the control coefficient settings for a method and <math id="S7.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{y_{1},y_{2},\ldots,y_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{y_{1},y_{2},\ldots,y_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> the corresponding Cognitive Bias Index (CBI) values. We formulate the metrics as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S7.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S7.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Monotonicity</span>: evaluates whether stronger interventions consistently move outputs in the intended direction.
<span id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Soft monotonicity</span> allows minor deviations by comparing actual sequences to ideal monotonic ones, which is measured by NDCG:
<math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{NDCG}=\frac{\text{DCG}}{\text{IDCG}},\ \text{DCG}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{rel_{i}}{\log_{2}(i+2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>NDCG</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mtext>DCG</mtext><mtext>IDCG</mtext></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mtext>DCG</mtext><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{NDCG}=\frac{\text{DCG}}{\text{IDCG}},\ \text{DCG}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{rel_{i}}{\log_{2}(i+2)}</annotation></semantics></math>,
with <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="rel_{i}=\exp(\alpha\cdot\frac{y_{actual}[i]-y_{ideal}[i]}{y_{range}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">rel_{i}=\exp(\alpha\cdot\frac{y_{actual}[i]-y_{ideal}[i]}{y_{range}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Strict monotonicity</span> (Spearman’s <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>) measures exact rank preservation:
<math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=1-\frac{6\sum d_{i}^{2}}{n(n^{2}-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle scriptlevel="-1"><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mstyle></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=1-\frac{6\sum d_{i}^{2}}{n(n^{2}-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the rank difference between <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Smoothness</span>: quantifies the stability of control adjustments.
<span id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">First-order smoothness</span> (<math id="S7.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>) captures local stability as the mean absolute difference of adjacent bias values:
<math id="S7.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{1}=\text{Mean}(|y_{i+1}-y_{i}|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Mean</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{1}=\text{Mean}(|y_{i+1}-y_{i}|)</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Second-order smoothness</span> (<math id="S7.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) captures trend continuity as the mean absolute difference of consecutive first-order changes:
<math id="S7.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{2}=\text{Mean}(|(y_{i+1}-y_{i})-(y_{i}-y_{i-1})|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Mean</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{2}=\text{Mean}(|(y_{i+1}-y_{i})-(y_{i}-y_{i-1})|)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Expressiveness</span>: measures the effective control range, defined as the difference between maximum and minimum observed bias levels:
<math id="S7.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Expressiveness}=\max(y_{i})-\min(y_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Expressiveness</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Expressiveness}=\max(y_{i})-\min(y_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.2 </span>Does the behavior pattern of an agent regulated by CoBRA transfer across tasks?</h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A central hypothesis behind CoBRA is that ”aligning an agent’s behavior with target profiles in a set of classical experiments will reliably induce the corresponding cognitive biases, which will then generalize and manifest across a broader range of tasks”. We study this transferrability problem by testing CoBRA across different classic social experimental paradigms for Framing Effect.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setup.</span> We test whether Control Coefficients calibrated on the <span id="S7.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Investment/Insurance</span> paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kahneman &amp; Tversky (2013)</a></cite> (individuals are more likely to purchase insurance when risks are described as potential losses rather than as missed gains) generalize to the <span id="S7.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Asian Disease</span> paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tversky &amp; Kahneman (1981)</a></cite> (Participants are more risk-averse under positive frames and more risk-seeking under loss frames). We select 10 diverse agent personas from the persona pools in Generative Agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al. (2023)</a></cite>, with varied specifications. For each persona, we sweep over all possible Control Coefficients. For each coefficient <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, we measure the resulting CBI in both the <span id="S7.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Investment/Insurance Paradigm</span> and the <span id="S7.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Asian Disease Paradigm</span>. This generates paired CBI sequences, <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(y_{k}^{\text{invest}},y_{k}^{\text{asian}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi><mtext>invest</mtext></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi><mtext>asian</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y_{k}^{\text{invest}},y_{k}^{\text{asian}})</annotation></semantics></math>, for each persona across the two experiments.
We also evaluate CoBRA using the Behavioral Regulation Engine, activating only one method at a time.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13588v1/persona_cbi_consistency.svg" id="S7.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:274/137;" width="274" height="137"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Reproducibility experiments across paradigms. The x-axis represents the Cognitive Bias Index measured in the <span id="S7.F7.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Investment/Insurance Paradigm</span>, while the y-axis shows the Cognitive Bias Index using the same control coefficients in the <span id="S7.F7.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Asian Disease Paradigm</span>. Each thin line represents a unique agent persona, and the bold line is the average of them. Control Coefficients calibrated on the <span id="S7.F7.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Investment/Insurance Paradigm</span> produce a linearly correlated CBI response in the <span id="S7.F7.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Asian Disease Paradigm</span>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S7.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span> The averaged trajectory curves demonstrate complete linear correlation between paradigms (Fig. <a href="#S7.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 7.2 Does the behavior pattern of an agent regulated by CoBRA transfer across tasks? ‣ 7 Technical benchmark ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>). This linear relationship indicates that control strength transfers proportionally: agents exhibiting high framing susceptibility in <span id="S7.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Investment/Insurance Paradigm</span> show correspondingly high susceptibility in <span id="S7.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Asian Disease Paradigm</span>. The CBI is generalizable across experimental paradigms.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.3 </span>Reproducibility</h3>

<div id="S7.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reproducible bias control across different model configurations is fundamental for scientific rigor in social science simulation research. We ensure that when specifying a particular level of cognitive bias, the LLM-based agent will produce equivalent behavioral tendencies regardless of the foundation model, sampling temperature, or whether reasoning is used. Without this reproducibility guarantee, results can’t be reliably replicated, compared, or validated—undermining the credibility of LLM-based social research.</p>
</div>
