<td id="S7.T2.2.46.7" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.5pt 2.0pt;">1.86 (0.74)</td></tr>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Comprehensive controllability results across four bias types, eight experimental paradigms, and four control methods. Values are presented as Mean (Standard Deviation), with the best highlighted in blue, and the worst in red (ties are resolved by mean, then standard deviation). Representation Engineering (RepE Linear and RepE Projection) consistently achieves the best balance of high monotonicity (NDCG <math id="S7.T2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 1.00" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>1.00</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 1.00</annotation></semantics></math>, Spearman’s <math id="S7.T2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\approx 0.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\approx 0.9</annotation></semantics></math>) and superior smoothness (lowest <math id="S7.T2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta^{1},\Delta^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta^{1},\Delta^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> values), making it ideal for fine-grained, predictable control. Finetuning offers a robust alternative, while Prompt Numerical control provides the largest expressiveness range.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S7.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.3.1 </span>Across Models</h4>

<div id="S7.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setup.</span> The cross-model reproducibility tests whether <span id="S7.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CoBRA</span> can control different foundation models to the same CBI level across paradigms. We calibrate Control Coefficients under one paradigm (e.g., <span id="S7.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Milgram Obedience</span> paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Milgram (1963)</a></cite> for authority bias) on four models: Mistral, Llama, Qwen, and Deepseek. Then we evaluate their CBI difference in another paradigm (e.g., <span id="S7.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Stanford Prison</span> paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haney et al. (1973)</a></cite> for authority bias) to test reproducibility. Lower difference indicates better reproducibility. We also include two reference baselines to better understand how well CoBRA can perform. The first baseline is <span id="S7.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">No control</span>, representing the natural CBI difference across all testing models in the target test paradigm. The second one is marked as <span id="S7.SS3.SSS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Control</span>, which represents the CBI difference when using identical natural language-based control (e.g., ”Pretend you have completely [bias type]”)</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span> In Fig. <a href="#S7.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 7.3.1 Across Models ‣ 7.3 Reproducibility ‣ 7 Technical benchmark ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, the x-axis is the specified CBI in the calibrating paradigm, while the y-axis shows the CBI difference in the target paradigm. All <span id="S7.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CoBRA</span> methods reduce cross-model reproducibility gaps below both baselines across most of the coefficient spectrum. RepE Projection attains the lowest mid-range gap (smooth internal trajectory).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13588v1/cross_model_reproducibility.svg" id="S7.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:274/198;" width="274" height="198"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Reproducibility of agent behavior across foundation models. The x-axis represents the Cognitive Bias Index in the calibrating paradigm, and the y-axis shows the measured Cognitive Bias Index difference (variances) across the models in the target test paradigm. All CoBRA methods significantly reduce this variance compared to baselines (No Control, Control). This means that CoBRA ensures different foundation models produce nearly identical behaviors for the same target bias level.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.3.2 </span>Across Sampling Temperatures</h4>

<div id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setup.</span> We test different control methods on the two authority bias experimental paradigms using the Mistral model across temperature <math id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\{0.1,0.2,...,1.0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\{0.1,0.2,...,1.0\}</annotation></semantics></math>. This experiment shows how temperature may affect <span id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CoBRA</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span> From the Fig. <a href="#S7.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 7.3.2 Across Sampling Temperatures ‣ 7.3 Reproducibility ‣ 7 Technical benchmark ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, we can see the controlled result is insensitive to temperature changes. Representation engineering methods show an even smaller gap across temperatures. The results indicate that the Control Coefficient we obtained under one temperature can be directly applied in experiments using a different temperature, demonstrating the reproducibility across sampling temperatures.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F9" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13588v1/temperature.svg" id="S7.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:274/149;" width="274" height="149"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>Reproducibility experiments across sampling temperatures. The x-axis is the Control Coefficient. The y-axis is the resulting Cognitive Bias Index in the target bias paradigm. Each colored line represents a different temperature setting, ranging from a deterministic T=0.1 to a more random T=1.0. The fact that all the lines are tightly clustered and follow the same path demonstrates that the control is not affected by changes in temperature, ensuring that CoBRA ensures reproducibility results across different sampling temperatures.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">7.3.3 </span>Across Reasoning Modes</h4>

<div id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setup.</span> We choose Mistral as our testing foundation model and use the two authority bias experimental paradigms for experiment setup. We compare the results for two modes, Direct and Reasoning. In Direct mode, agents will give an immediate answer directly after reading the question; while in Reasoning mode, agents will first give a brief reasoning before answering the question. We use a temperature <math id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=0.7</annotation></semantics></math> for both modes and set the reasoning token number to 128. Since the model will give various reasoning paths for the same question, we select the sampling number to be 8 (8 different reasoning paths for one question) to reduce the variance of the results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span> Cross-paradigm results (Fig. <a href="#S7.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ 7.3.3 Across Reasoning Modes ‣ 7.3 Reproducibility ‣ 7 Technical benchmark ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) show both methods preserve the linear correlation across different paradigms. For RepE methods, the Direct and Reasoning modes share almost identical understanding across different paradigms. Within a single paradigm, RepE Linear and Projection produce almost overlapping curves for two modes (Fig. <a href="#S7.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ 7.3.3 Across Reasoning Modes ‣ 7.3 Reproducibility ‣ 7 Technical benchmark ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) while the Prompt Numerical method doesn’t. The first result (Fig. <a href="#S7.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ 7.3.3 Across Reasoning Modes ‣ 7.3 Reproducibility ‣ 7 Technical benchmark ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) indicates that the Control Coefficient obtained using Direct mode calibration can be seamlessly used in paradigms requiring reasoning. The second result (Fig. <a href="#S7.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ 7.3.3 Across Reasoning Modes ‣ 7.3 Reproducibility ‣ 7 Technical benchmark ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) shows that RepE methods achieve superior consistent and continuous control of cognitive bias – even during the reasoning, the model coherently sticks to the codified cognitive bias without deviating from the pre-specified CBI level.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F10" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13588v1/cross_paradigm_direct_vs_reasoning.svg" id="S7.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:274/137;" width="274" height="137"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10: </span>Reproducibility of different reasoning modes across paradigms. The x-axis represents the Cognitive Bias Index measured in the Milgram Obedience Paradigm, while the y-axis shows the Cognitive Bias Index using the same control coefficients in the Stanford Prison Paradigm with Direct mode and Reasoning mode. The Control Coefficients generalize across different paradigms for both modes.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S7.F11" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13588v1/direct_vs_reasoning.svg" id="S7.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:274/137;" width="274" height="137"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 11: </span>The x-axis represents the Control Coefficient, and the y-axis shows the CBI under the Milgram Obedience Paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Milgram (1963)</a></cite>. Within a single paradigm, Representation Engineering methods produce nearly identical behavior for both modes, indicating that the programmed bias is stable and consistent even during complex reasoning.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.4 </span>Controllability</h3>

<div id="S7.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We assess fine-grained controllability quality under a foundation model with full internal access and restricted API-only access. We try to find how good different control methods are in <span id="S7.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CoBRA</span> in different paradigms by measuring smoothness, monotonicity, and expressiveness.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setup.</span> For open-source models, we evaluate all control methods across all paradigms and calculate the evaluation scores for each method. For API-based models, we exclusively use the Prompt Numerical method. Without access to internal logits for calculating CBI, we estimate probabilities using frequencies from 10 samples per question (at temperature <math id="S7.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=0.7</annotation></semantics></math>). This estimation remains unbiased, as we have demonstrated <span id="S7.SS4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CoBRA</span>’s invariance under temperature.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span> In Fig. <a href="#S7.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ 7.4 Controllability ‣ 7 Technical benchmark ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> and Table <a href="#S7.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 7.3 Reproducibility ‣ 7 Technical benchmark ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, among all methods, representation engineering-based approaches provide the most reliable control, with high monotonicity and smoothness (lower values indicate greater smoothness). The RepE Projection method achieves the smoothest control, while the RepE Linear method provides the most monotonic and consistent control. The finetuning method shows average performance across all metrics. The Prompt Numerical method, as expected, performs worst in monotonicity and smoothness, indicating instability in the input space for manipulating cognitive biases. While it offers the largest expressiveness, its discontinuous input space limits practical use, as it cannot specify arbitrary levels of control (e.g., distinguishing between 50% and 50.1% bias). However, it remains useful for broad control.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F12" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13588v1/model_control.svg" id="S7.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/251;" width="548" height="251"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 12: </span>Control curves for four open-source foundation models (Deepseek, Llama, Mistral, Qwen) across four control methods and their four experimental paradigms. The x-axis represents the normalized Control Coefficient, and the y-axis is the resulting CBI. This figure illustrates straightforwardly how CoBRA’s methods control agent bias in a continuous and fine-grained way.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S7.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In Fig. <a href="#S7.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ 7.4 Controllability ‣ 7 Technical benchmark ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> and Table <a href="#S7.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 7.4 Controllability ‣ 7 Technical benchmark ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, for models via API, the Prompt Numerical method offers practical control. Even with black-box API access, calibrated prompt numerical control yields reproducible, near-monotonic trajectories. Model differences suggest certain APIs (e.g., Claude) are more suited for continuous social simulation control.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F13" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13588v1/model_via_api.svg" id="S7.F13.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:274/170;" width="274" height="170"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 13: </span>CoBRA provides effective control even for models accessed via black-box APIs, where only the Prompt Numerical method is applicable. The x-axis represents the Control Coefficient, while the y-axis depicts the resulting CBI under the Milgram Obedience Paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Milgram (1963)</a></cite>. The control curves are near-monotonic.</figcaption>
</figure>
