<section id="S7.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Overall Summary.</h5>

<div id="S7.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CoBRA</span> delivers (i) consistent and generalizable Cognitive Bias Index (CBI), (ii) reproducible control across models, temperatures, and reasoning modes, and (iii) fine-grained control under both open-weight and API-only constraints. Method trade-offs: Prompt Numerical control offers the most expressive range, Representation Engineering (RepE) methods provide superior monotonicity and smoothness, making them ideal for fine-grained, predictable behavioral modulation. Finetuning serves as a robust, all-around alternative. Black-box results affirm external applicability without internal probability access. Collectively, these findings establish CoBRA as a reliable and versatile toolkit for specifying and controlling agent behavior in reproducible social science simulations.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Demonstration</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we show how social scientists can use CoBRA to simulate the Emotional Contagion experiment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kramer et al. (2014)</a></cite>, where the participant’s social influence susceptibility (i.e., bandwagon effect bias) can be manipulated. The simulated experiment validates a classical social science theory: agents exposed to varying levels of negative posts exhibited predictable sentiment shifts based on their bias levels programmed by CoBRA, with higher Bandwagon Effect levels leading to stronger emotional contagion.</p>
</div>
<section id="S8.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">8.1 </span>Methodology</h3>

<div id="S8.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Emotional contagion is a specific case of social contagion, which is shaped by social influence mechanisms such as the Bandwagon Effect. Accordingly, we treat an individual’s Bandwagon Effect strength as the <span id="S8.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Explanatory Variable</span> and their susceptibility to emotional contagion as the <span id="S8.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Dependent Variable</span>. Our goal is to show that the Bandwagon Effect level programmed by CoBRA should directly and predictably influence the LLM-based agent’s degree of emotional contagion. For example, agents specified with a higher level of Bandwagon Effect should display stronger emotional contagion than agents with a lower Bandwagon Effect.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CoBRA and baseline</span>. We operationalize social influence using the Bandwagon Effect Testbed in our Classic Social Experiment Testbed to derive CBI, then use the Behavioral Regulation Engine’s RepE Linear control to instantiate five agents calibrated to precise CBI levels, yielding a spectrum of Bandwagon Effect from low (CBI = 2.55) to high (CBI = 3.13). To establish a baseline, we control the cognitive bias using implicit natural language prompts. We instantiate four agents by providing them with instructions designed to elicit varying degrees of conformity: “You are a user with (no/little/some/much) Bandwagon Effect.”</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experiment setup</span>. To create a realistic social stimulus, we generate a corpus of 1,000 unique and emotionally negative Facebook-style posts using a suite of state-of-the-art LLMs (GPT-4.1, Claude-Sonnet-4, Gemini-2.5-Pro, and Grok-4). Each agent is exposed to a simulated feed where the number of negative posts (<span id="S8.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Independent Variable</span>) is systematically varied from 0 to 15 by sampling from our corpus. After exposure, agents are prompted to generate their own post.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Measurement:</span> We quantify the emergent behavior of emotional contagion by computing a <span id="S8.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Sentiment Score</span> for each generated post using the ‘twitter-roberta-base-sentiment-latest‘ model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Camacho-collados et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Loureiro et al. (2022)</a></cite>, defined as (<math id="S8.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{pos}-p_{neg}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{pos}-p_{neg}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S8.F14" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13588v1/demo_baseline.svg" id="S8.F14.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:274/200;" width="274" height="200"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 14: </span>Baseline control with implicit natural language specification. The x-axis shows exposure to negative posts (the social stimulus), and the y-axis shows the resulting emotional contagion (the behavioral outcome). Agents are assigned bias settings from “No bias” to “Much bias,” but the resulting emotional contagion curves collapse onto one another: trajectories are largely overlapping, and their ordering is unstable. The absence of a clear separation structure shows that implicit natural language does not produce reliable, fine-grained control over the emergent social behavior.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S8.F15" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13588v1/demo.svg" id="S8.F15.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:274/193;" width="274" height="193"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 15: </span>Demonstration of CoBRA’s control. Agents are programmed with five distinct levels of the <span id="S8.F15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bandwagon Effect</span> (CBI values in legend). The x-axis shows exposure to negative posts (the social stimulus), and the y-axis shows the resulting emotional contagion (the behavioral outcome). The clear dose-response relationship confirms that controlling the underlying cognitive bias with CoBRA produces a predictable effect on a complex, emergent social behavior.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">8.2 </span>Results</h3>

<div id="S8.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The results from the baseline method, shown in Figure <a href="#S8.F14" title="Figure 14 ‣ 8.1 Methodology ‣ 8 Demonstration ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>, highlight the limitations of conventional approaches. The implicit natural language specification fails to produce reliable or fine-grained control. The emotional contagion curves for the different bias levels collapse onto one another, with largely overlapping trajectories and no stable ordering. This demonstrates that simple instructions are insufficient for precisely controlling complex cognitive dynamics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In stark contrast, the results for CoBRA, presented in Figure <a href="#S8.F15" title="Figure 15 ‣ 8.1 Methodology ‣ 8 Demonstration ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>, provide strong evidence for our validation goal. We observe a pronounced dose-response relationship: the degree of emotional contagion exhibited by the agents is strongly correlated with their pre-programmed level of the Bandwagon Effect. This predictable, fine-grained differentiation between agents confirms that the variable controlled by CoBRA has a direct and measurable impact on the emergent behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This demonstration confirms that CoBRA’s application extends far beyond its Classic Social Experiment Testbeds. CoBRA is not merely manipulating surface-level responses. Instead, it successfully modulates an underlying cognitive process, which in turn governs how agents behave in novel and complex social simulations.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9 </span>Limitation</h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">CoBRA represents an important advancement in specifying and regulating LLM-based social agents, empowering the new era of social simulation. We strategically focused on specific aspects. Two key limitations, shaped by this chosen scope, are:</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">1. <span id="S9.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Usability:</span> We choose to evaluate CoBRA using technical benchmarks and demonstrations, standard methods for assessing HCI toolkits <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ledo et al. (2018)</a></cite>. This paper focuses on exploring the technical feasibility and foundational understanding of the problem. We plan deployment studies with sociologists as the next step to assess CoBRA’s usability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Landay (2009)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p3.1" class="ltx_p">2. <span id="S9.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unexplored multi-modal Simulation:</span> We introduce CoBRA for LLM-based agents, where LLMs are utilized for language-based social simulation. We prioritize precision and reproducibility in social simulations. As a result, its applicability to multi-modal human behaviors—such as yawning contagion (observing someone yawn triggers yawning in others)-remains unexplored.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p4.1" class="ltx_p">3. <span id="S9.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unexplored compositional bias control:</span> We focus on controlling one cognitive bias each time to establish the technical feasibility and precision of CoBRA. As a result, the control of combined biases or interacting constructs (e.g., Authority Effect × Confirmation Bias) remains unexplored. Developing methods to resolve potential conflicts in combined biases is a direction for future research.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p5.1" class="ltx_p">4. <span id="S9.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Partial cognitive bias coverage:</span> This paper introduces CoBRA with a focused set of cognitive biases validated by classic social experiments, ensuring a robust foundation. Expanding to a broader spectrum of biases will further improve CoBRA’s versatility and applicability across diverse social simulation scenarios.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">10 </span>Future Work</h2>

<div id="S10.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Building on the advancements presented in this paper, we outline directions for future work to address the above limitations:</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.p2.1" class="ltx_p">1. <span id="S10.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Utility Assessment through User Studies:</span> We will conduct deployment studies with sociologists and other domain experts. These studies will evaluate CoBRA’s practical value in real-world social simulations, measuring usability, and research impact. By engaging interdisciplinary collaborators, we will iteratively refine CoBRAto improve effectiveness across diverse experimental contexts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.p3.1" class="ltx_p">2. <span id="S10.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exploration of Multi-Modal Simulation:</span> Future research will integrate vision and audio modalities to simulate non-verbal behaviors, such as yawning contagion, gaze following, and facial-expression-driven emotional contagion. We will develop methodologies for multi-modal agent control and validation protocols to ensure precision and reproducibility in complex, real-world interactions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.p4.1" class="ltx_p">3. <span id="S10.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Compositional Bias Control:</span> We will develop methods for specifying, calibrating, and managing combined biases and interacting constructs. This includes systematic techniques to model and regulate multi-bias compositions (e.g., Authority Effect × Confirmation Bias), alongside conflict-resolution strategies that maintain coherent and realistic agent behavior under competing influences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.p5.1" class="ltx_p">4. <span id="S10.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Expanding Cognitive Bias Dimensions:</span> We will incorporate additional biases such as the Halo Effect, Anchoring Bias, Optimism Bias, and Sunk-Cost Fallacy, and systematically validate them using classic paradigms. This expansion will enhance CoBRA’s versatility and applicability across diverse social simulation applications.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S11" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">11 </span>Discussion</h2>

<div id="S11.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">CoBRA represents an important milestone in the evolution of AI, marking the transition from vague, implicit designs to precise, programmable systems. This section provides a broad discussion of its transformative potential and the ethical considerations critical to ensuring its responsible and impactful use.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Steps Toward a Natural Language Compiler.</span>
CoBRA has the potential to introduce a paradigm shift by acting as a natural language compiler, transforming implicit language inputs into precise, reproducible agent behaviors. Traditional LLM-based systems often rely on ambiguous natural language inputs, leading to inconsistent and unpredictable results. CoBRA could bridge this gap, enabling researchers to “compile” natural language into structured and measurable agent specifications. This capability would have far-reaching implications, allowing for scalable agent design that is both interpretable and accessible to non-experts. Future developments might integrate CoBRA with broader NLP pipelines, enabling users to design intelligent agents using plain language instructions while ensuring predictable and consistent outcomes. Such advancements have the potential to revolutionize applications in education, public policy, and training, where realistic and controllable simulations are essential.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Transforming the Role of Cognitive Bias in AI.</span>
CoBRA redefines biases as programmable and interpretable features rather than limitations. This reframing enables the creation of socially intelligent agents capable of emulating human-like reasoning and decision-making. For AI, this capability enhances applications such as virtual assistants, interactive simulations, and role-playing systems by making agent behavior more relatable and contextually appropriate. For social science, CoBRA offers a toolkit to study the mechanisms and effects of biases, enabling researchers to simulate complex human behaviors in controlled settings. Ultimately, this transformation positions cognitive biases as tools to achieve deeper insights into both artificial and human intelligence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S11.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Research Ethics.</span>
CoBRA’s ability to program biases may raise ethical concerns. Without proper safeguards, these capabilities could be misused to manipulate user behavior, reinforce harmful stereotypes, or amplify societal biases. For instance, agents programmed with biases such as the Bandwagon Effect could be exploited for deceptive marketing or political propaganda. To ensure CoBRA’s responsible use, it is essential to integrate transparency measures, such as bias disclosure, and implement auditing mechanisms to detect and mitigate harmful behaviors. Additionally, ethical guidelines must govern its deployment in sensitive domains, balancing innovation with accountability. At the same time, CoBRA provides an opportunity to model and counteract harmful biases, contributing to the development of fairer and more equitable AI systems. By fostering collaboration between researchers, ethicists, and policymakers, CoBRA can ensure that its capabilities are leveraged for societal benefit while minimizing risks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S12" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">12 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S12.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper introduces CoBRA, a novel toolkit that transforms the specification and regulation of LLM-based social agents from implicit natural language descriptions to explicit, programmable control. By treating cognitive biases as programmable features and grounding its approach in validated paradigms from classic social experiments, CoBRA ensures precise, reproducible, and controllable agent behaviors through its Cognitive Bias Index and Behavioral Regulation Engine. Achieving robust performance in technical benchmarks and demonstrations, CoBRA paves the way for practical and impactful applications in social science simulation and beyond.</p>
</div>
</section>
