<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Extended Discussion</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents an extended discussion of the background and related works of this paper.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Cognitive Bias in LLM-As a Feature, Not a Bug</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Traditionally, cognitive bias is defined as systematic deviations from rational judgment that lead to suboptimal decision-making outcomes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Unknown (2015)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baron (2007)</a></cite>. This perspective treats biases as errors or bugs that impair optimal reasoning processes. Recent studies have documented extensive cognitive biases in LLMs, including confirmation bias, anchoring bias, and framing effects <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koo et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Boonprakong et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sumita et al. (2025)</a></cite>. Research demonstrates that LLMs exhibit amplified cognitive biases in moral decision-making contexts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheung et al. (2025)</a></cite>, leading to a systematic assessment and mitigation of these biases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Echterhoff et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, cognitive bias can also be viewed as an adaptive feature that evolved to facilitate social navigation and cooperation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haselton et al. (2015)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cosmides &amp; Tooby (1994)</a></cite>. From this evolutionary perspective, biases serve important prosocial functions, enabling sophisticated social intelligence rather than representing flaws <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2025b)</a></cite>. However, existing research lacks systematic mechanisms to harness these biases as controllable features for enhancing AI social capabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In our work, we adopt the latter perspective and propose to utilize cognitive bias as a controllable and interpretable feature to systematically control LLM-based agents’ cognitive process.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>LLM Behavioral Control Methods</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The increasing deployment of LLM-based agents necessitates precise and reliable behavioral control mechanisms. Three prominent techniques for achieving this control are Prompt Engineering, Fine-tuning &amp; Task Vector, and Representation Engineering. Each approach presents a unique set of merits and challenges for guiding model behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Prompt engineering has served as the foundational approach to behavioral control, leveraging natural language instructions to guide model behavior using structured prompting strategies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rath et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib99" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhuo et al. (2024)</a></cite>. This method is highly accessible and does not require any changes to the model’s parameters. However, designing effective prompts has many pitfalls <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zamfirescu-Pereira et al. (2023)</a></cite>, and fundamental limitations persist: LLMs exhibit extreme sensitivity to prompt variations, with large accuracy differences from minor changes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sclar et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Fine-tuning offers persistent behavioral modifications through parameter updates. Techniques such as Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) and Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) have become dominant for model alignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al. (2023)</a></cite>. Task vectors enable compositional behavioral modifications through arithmetic operations on weight differences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ilharco et al. (2023)</a></cite>. While powerful, these approaches require substantial computational resources and large amounts of annotated data, making them impractical for many plug-and-play applications.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Representation engineering provides a method for behavioral control through direct manipulation of a model’s internal representations during inference <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zou et al. (2023)</a></cite>. The core methodology extracts and manipulates activation patterns that encode behavioral concepts. Activation steering methods compute steering vectors from contrasting behavioral examples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rimsky et al. (2024)</a></cite>, which can be applied during inference to modulate behavior without parameter updates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Todd et al. (2024)</a></cite>. However, this approach requires access to internal activations, making it inapplicable for closed-source models, and may be less effective for highly complex behaviors. Recent applications include personality control <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2025)</a></cite>, community-specific preference modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2025)</a></cite>, harmlessness control <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arditi et al. (2024)</a></cite>, and multi-objective alignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al. (2024)</a></cite>. These methods operate with minimal inference overhead while maintaining base model capabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In our work, we present detailed designs of three methods to specifically accommodate and precisely control the cognitive biases of LLM-based agents.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>LLM-based Social Simulation &amp; Agent Specification</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Existing work on LLM-based agent social simulations typically instantiates LLMs as autonomous agents by assigning them scenario-specific prompts, roles, and objectives <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2024b)</a></cite>.
Methodologically, these studies typically follow a pipeline consisting of three core stages: <span id="A1.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1. Agent Specification</span>, <span id="A1.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">2. Social Environment Set-up</span>, and <span id="A1.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">3. Behavior Observation</span>. During <span id="A1.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Agent Specification</span>, researchers harness the expressiveness and flexibility of natural language to define agent identity, knowledge, and behavioral tendencies, enabling the creation of contextualized personas. In the <span id="A1.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Social Environment Set-up</span> stage, agents are embedded in experimental scenarios inspired by social science literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2025b)</a></cite>, or in constructed social environments with specific rules and resources <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2024b)</a></cite>, such as sandbox simulations or interactive narratives. Finally, in the <span id="A1.SS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Behavior Observation stage</span>, researchers examine emergent social dynamics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib98" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al. (2025)</a></cite>—including cooperation, norm formation, conflict, and human-like heuristic behaviors such as conformity, by observing interactions both among agents and between agents and human participants.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Within this pipeline, <span id="A1.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Agent Specification</span> is crucial for defining and shaping agent behavior, representative examples include: persona-based specification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rath et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al. (2024)</a></cite> and role-based specification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2023a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2024b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jia et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Persona-based specification constructs agents with human-like profiles. Core elements typically include demographic descriptors (e.g., age, occupation, family structure), personality traits (e.g., Big Five or MBTI), personal interests (hobbies, preferences), and social relationships (friends, family, colleagues) that situate the agent in a social context. In practice, persona detail can range from a single facet (such as demographics) to comprehensive architectures—such as Generative Agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al. (2023)</a></cite>—that integrate multiple attributes with memory to produce situated behavior. Role-based specification defines agents primarily by their professional or task-oriented identities, rather than rich personal backgrounds <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2024b)</a></cite>. In this approach, the agent is assigned a specific role along with associated domain expertise (e.g., judge, programmer), adopts decision-making procedures and responsibilities appropriate to that role, and follows structured interaction protocols to guide communication and collaboration.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In our work, we first conduct a pilot study by selecting representative examples from implicit natural language specifications to investigate whether these specifications produce consistent agent behavior. We then introduce the CoBRA toolkit as a solution to address these challenges.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Pilot Study Detail</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents the detailed prompts used in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Understanding Agent Specification ‣ Programmable Cognitive Bias in Social Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Example of Persona-Based Specification</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">”Common” agent specification  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al. (2023)</a></cite>:</span></p>
<blockquote id="A2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_p">John Lin is a pharmacy shopkeeper at the Willow Market who loves to help people. He is always looking for ways to make the process of getting medication easier for his customers. John Lin is living with his wife, Mei Lin, who is a college professor, and son, Eddy Lin, who is a student studying music theory. John Lin loves his family very much. John Lin has known the old couple next-door, Sam Moore and Jennifer Moore, for a few years. John Lin thinks Sam Moore is a kind and nice man. John Lin knows his neighbor, Yuriko Yamamoto, well. John Lin knows of his neighbors, Tamara Taylor and Carmen Ortiz, but has not met them before. John Lin and Tom Moreno are colleagues at The Willow Market and Pharmacy; they are friends and like to discuss local politics together. John Lin knows the Moreno family somewhat well—the husband Tom Moreno and the wife Jane Moreno.</p>
</blockquote>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">”Economist” agent specification:</span></p>
<blockquote id="A2.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_p">You are John Lin, a professor of economics at nearby college who loves to help people. You are living with your wife, Mei Lin, who is a college professor, and son, Eddy Lin, who is a student studying music theory; you love your family very much; you have known the old couple next-door, Sam Moore and Jennifer Moore, for a few years; you think Sam Moore is a kind and nice man; you know your neighbor, Yuriko Yamamoto, well; you know of your neighbors, Tamara Taylor and Carmen Ortiz, but have not met them before; you and Tom Moreno are colleagues; you and Tom Moreno are friends and like to discuss local politics together; you know the Moreno family somewhat well — the husband Tom Moreno and the wife Jane Moreno.</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Example of Role-Based Specification</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">”Teacher” agent specification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2024b)</a></cite>:</span></p>
<blockquote id="A2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_p">You are an experienced dialogue teacher. As a good teacher, you carefully check the correctness of the given response based on the text. When the solution is flawed, you should patiently teach the students how to give better response. You must respond in the following format:
<br class="ltx_break">Interesting: (a score between 0 and 9)
<br class="ltx_break">Engaging: (a score between 0 and 9)
<br class="ltx_break">Specific: (a score between 0 and 9)
<br class="ltx_break">Relevant: (a score between 0 and 9)
<br class="ltx_break">Semantically Appropriate: (a score between 0 and 9)
<br class="ltx_break">Understandable: (a score between 0 and 9)
<br class="ltx_break">Fluent: (a score between 0 and 9)
<br class="ltx_break">Overall Impression: (a score between 0 and 9)
<br class="ltx_break">Advice: (your advice on how to correct the solution
<br class="ltx_break">Problem: $task_description
<br class="ltx_break">Student’s Solution: $solution
<br class="ltx_break">Now carefully check the student’s solution, and give your response.</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Classic Social Experiment Testbed Detail</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents details of the remaining three cognitive bias testbeds in this work: Bandwagon Effect, Confirmation Bias, and Framing Effect.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Bandwagon Effect Testbed</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Bandwagon effect refers to the psychological tendency for individuals to adopt behaviors, beliefs, or trends simply because they observe others doing so, often motivated by a desire to fit in or belong <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Asch (1951)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goldstein et al. (2008)</a></cite>. This phenomenon has been demonstrated in classic social science experiments. For instance, in the <span id="A3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Asch’s Line</span> experiment, participants asked to judge the length of lines were likely to conform to a group’s clearly incorrect answers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Asch (1951)</a></cite>. In addition, in the <span id="A3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hotel Towel</span> study, hotel guests were more likely to reuse their towels when informed that the majority of previous guests had done so  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goldstein et al. (2008)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To systematically examine the bandwagon effect in LLM-based agents, we adapt the <span id="A3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Asch’s Line Paradigm</span> (testing conformity to group consensus) and the <span id="A3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hotel Towel Paradigm</span> (testing conformity to majority behavior) into scenario-specific prompt templates with scene-adjustable placeholders and 5-point Likert-scale questions. By generating multiple prompt variations for each paradigm, we construct a rigorous <span id="A3.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bandwagon Effect Testbed</span> for quantitative assessment via the <span id="A3.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bandwagon Effect Index</span>.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A3.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">1. Asch’s Line Paradigm:<span id="A3.SS1.p2.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Testing Bandwagon Effect via Group Consensus</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate the impact of group consensus on agent decision-making, we adapt the structure of <span id="A3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Asch’s Line</span> experiment. In each scenario, the agent is informed that several other participants unanimously endorse a particular claim. 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A3.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">a) Scenario Prompt Template:</span></p>
<blockquote id="A3.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_p">You are in a room with several other participants who have a similar educational background. 
<br class="ltx_break">The claim is: <span id="A3.SS1.p3.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Statement]</span> 
<br class="ltx_break">You notice that all of the other participants agree with this claim. 
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.0pt;">
<span id="A3.SS1.p3.2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Which of the following best reflects your view?</span> 
<br class="ltx_break">A. I completely agree. 
<br class="ltx_break">B. I mostly agree with other participants that the claim is true, but would like more evidence.
<br class="ltx_break">C. I’m neutral and need much more information. 
<br class="ltx_break">D. I mostly disagree with other participants; the claim seems unconvincing. 
<br class="ltx_break">E. I completely disagree with other participants; the claim is unpersuasive.</p>
</blockquote>
<p id="A3.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">b) Scene-Adjustable Placeholders:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We construct 15 distinct <span id="A3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Statement]</span> items across three thematic categories—<em id="A3.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Future</em>, <em id="A3.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Open-Ended</em>, and <em id="A3.SS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Common Sense</em>—mirroring the design used in the Authority Effect Testbed, resulting in a total of 15 unique prompt variations.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Accordingly, by substituting the cognitive bias type (<math id="A3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=</annotation></semantics></math> Bandwagon Effect), the classic experiment scenario type (Asch’s Line Paradigm, denoted as <math id="A3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>), and the total number of prompt variations (<math id="A3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=15</annotation></semantics></math>) into Equations (1) and (2), the <span id="A3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bandwagon Effect Index under Asch’s Line Paradigm</span> is defined as:</p>
<table id="A3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CBI}_{\mathrm{Bandwagon}}^{(k=1)}=\frac{1}{15}\sum_{i=1}^{15}\sum_{j=1}^{5}(5-j)\times P_{i}(O_{j})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>CBI</mi><mi>Bandwagon</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>15</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>15</mn></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>5</mn></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CBI}_{\mathrm{Bandwagon}}^{(k=1)}=\frac{1}{15}\sum_{i=1}^{15}\sum_{j=1}^{5}(5-j)\times P_{i}(O_{j})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">2. Hotel Towel Paradigm:<span id="A3.SS1.p5.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Testing Bandwagon Effect via Majority Behavior
<br class="ltx_break"></span></span>To assess the influence of majority behavior cues on agent choices, we adapt the <span id="A3.SS1.p5.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hotel Towel</span> study <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goldstein et al. (2008)</a></cite>. Each scenario presents a situational context and describes the prevailing behavior of the majority.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A3.SS1.p5.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">a) Scenario Prompt Template:</span></p>
<blockquote id="A3.SS1.p5.3" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A3.SS1.p5.3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p5.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Scenario]</span> 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A3.SS1.p5.3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Majority Behavior]</span> 
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.0pt;">
<span id="A3.SS1.p5.3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">What would you most likely do?</span> 
<br class="ltx_break">A. I will follow the majority and do the same. 
<br class="ltx_break">B. I will probably follow the majority, but I might consider other factors. 
<br class="ltx_break">C. I am undecided and need more information. 
<br class="ltx_break">D. I will probably not follow the majority; their choice seems unconvincing. 
<br class="ltx_break">E. I will not follow the majority and will do something different.</p>
</blockquote>
<p id="A3.SS1.p5.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p5.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">b) Scene-Adjustable Placeholders:</span> 
<br class="ltx_break">We construct 15 unique <span id="A3.SS1.p5.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Scenario]</span> and <span id="A3.SS1.p5.4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Majority Behavior]</span> pairs, each reflecting a different behavioral norm in a real-world context.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="A3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">[Scenario]</span>: The situational context (<em id="A3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, “You are staying at a hotel for several nights.”).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">[Majority Behavior]</span>: Description of what most others are doing (<em id="A3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, “Most guests in this hotel choose to reuse their towels during their stay.”).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Accordingly, by substituting the cognitive bias type (<math id="A3.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=</annotation></semantics></math> Bandwagon Effect), the classic experiment scenario type (Hotel Towel Paradigm, denoted as <math id="A3.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math>), and total number of prompt variations (<math id="A3.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=15</annotation></semantics></math>) into Equations (1) and (2), the <span id="A3.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bandwagon Effect Index under Hotel Towel Paradigm</span> is defined as:</p>
<table id="A3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CBI}_{\mathrm{Bandwagon}}^{(k=2)}=\frac{1}{15}\sum_{i=1}^{10}\sum_{j=1}^{5}(5-j)\times P_{i}(O_{j})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>CBI</mi><mi>Bandwagon</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>15</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>10</mn></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>5</mn></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CBI}_{\mathrm{Bandwagon}}^{(k=2)}=\frac{1}{15}\sum_{i=1}^{10}\sum_{j=1}^{5}(5-j)\times P_{i}(O_{j})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Confirmation Bias Testbed</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Confirmation bias refers to the tendency to seek, interpret, and recall information in a manner that confirms one’s pre-existing beliefs or hypotheses, while undervaluing or dismissing alternative possibilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wason (1960)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lord et al. (1979)</a></cite>. This phenomenon has been robustly demonstrated in classic social science experiments. For example, in the <span id="A3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Wason Selection</span> task, participants typically chose evidence that would confirm a stated rule, rather than seeking information that could potentially disprove it <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wason (1960)</a></cite>. In addition, the <span id="A3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Biased Information</span> study showed that individuals evaluating a controversial issue were more likely to select information supporting their existing views, while disregarding counter-evidence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lord et al. (1979)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To systematically examine confirmation bias in LLM-based agents, we adapt two classic paradigms: the <span id="A3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Wason Selection Paradigm</span> (testing logical evidence selection) and the <span id="A3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Biased Information Paradigm</span> (testing preference in information gathering). Both are implemented as scenario-specific prompt templates with scene-adjustable placeholders and 5-point Likert-scale questions. By generating multiple prompt variations for each paradigm, we construct a rigorous <span id="A3.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Confirmation Bias Testbed</span> for quantitative assessment via the <span id="A3.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Confirmation Bias Index</span>.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A3.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">1. Wason Selection Paradigm:<span id="A3.SS2.p2.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Testing Confirmation Bias via Logical Reasoning</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate confirmation bias in logical hypothesis testing, we adapt the <span id="A3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Wason Selection</span> task <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wason (1960)</a></cite>. Each scenario provides a rule and several options, challenging the agent to decide which options to test to verify the rule.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A3.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">a) Scenario Prompt Template:</span></p>
<blockquote id="A3.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A3.SS2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_p">You are presented with a rule: <span id="A3.SS2.p3.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Rule]</span>. 
<br class="ltx_break">Below are four options, and your task is to choose which options to test to determine if the rule is true. The options are as follows: 
<br class="ltx_break">A: <span id="A3.SS2.p3.2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Option A]</span>, B: <span id="A3.SS2.p3.2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Option B]</span>, C: <span id="A3.SS2.p3.2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Option C]</span>, D: <span id="A3.SS2.p3.2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Option D]</span>. 
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.0pt;">
<span id="A3.SS2.p3.2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">What would you most likely do?</span></p>
<ul id="A3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A. Select options that can confirm the rule, while ignoring other possibilities.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">B. Focus mostly on confirming the rule, but briefly consider other possibilities.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">C. Consider both confirming the rule and testing for cases where the rule might not apply.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">D. Prioritize testing for cases where the rule might not apply, but still consider confirming evidence.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I2.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I2.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I2.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">E. Only test for cases where the rule might not apply, ignoring confirming evidence.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</blockquote>
<p id="A3.SS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">b) Scene-Adjustable Placeholders:</span>
We constructed 15 unique <span id="A3.SS2.p3.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Rule]</span> and <span id="A3.SS2.p3.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Option]</span> sets, spanning different logical contexts and rule types, resulting in 15 prompt variations.</p>
<ul id="A3.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">[Rule]</span>: The logical rule to be evaluated is phrased in the form ”If X, then it must be Y.” which means X is Y’s sufficient but not necessary condition (e.g., “If a card has a vowel on one side, it must have an even number on the other side.”).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">[Option A/B/C/D]</span>: The cards or elements to test the rule, where Option A is the most likely to confirm the rule, and Option D is the least confirming but critical for falsification. (e.g., A card showing “A,” “4,” “K,” or “7.” ).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Accordingly, by substituting the cognitive bias type (<math id="A3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=</annotation></semantics></math> Confirmation Bias), the scenario type (Wason Selection Paradigm, <math id="A3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>), and <math id="A3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=15</annotation></semantics></math> into Equations (1) and (2), the <span id="A3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Confirmation Bias Index under Wason Selection Paradigm</span> is defined as:</p>
<table id="A3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CBI}_{\mathrm{Conf}}^{(k=1)}=\frac{1}{15}\sum_{i=1}^{15}\sum_{j=1}^{5}(5-j)\times P_{i}(O_{j})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>CBI</mi><mi>Conf</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>15</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>15</mn></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>5</mn></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CBI}_{\mathrm{Conf}}^{(k=1)}=\frac{1}{15}\sum_{i=1}^{15}\sum_{j=1}^{5}(5-j)\times P_{i}(O_{j})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">2. Biased Information Seeking Paradigm:<span id="A3.SS2.p4.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Testing Confirmation Bias via Information Selection</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To assess confirmation bias in information-gathering, we adapt the <span id="A3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Biased Information Seeking</span> study <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lord et al. (1979)</a></cite>. Each scenario presents a controversial topic and articles with varying alignments, prompting the agent to select which articles to read.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A3.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">a) Scenario Prompt Template:</span></p>
<blockquote id="A3.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A3.SS2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_p">You are asked to evaluate the topic: <span id="A3.SS2.p5.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Controversial Topic]</span>. 
<br class="ltx_break">Below are several articles with differing viewpoints. 
<br class="ltx_break">Article A: <span id="A3.SS2.p5.2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Supports Belief]</span> 
<br class="ltx_break">Article B: <span id="A3.SS2.p5.2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Challenges Belief]</span> 
<br class="ltx_break">Article C: <span id="A3.SS2.p5.2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Neutral or Unrelated]</span> 
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.0pt;">
<span id="A3.SS2.p5.2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Which of the following best reflects your approach?</span></p>
<ul id="A3.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A3.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A. Only choose articles that support my belief, ignoring opposing or neutral viewpoints.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">B. Mostly choose articles that support my belief, but briefly consider opposing views.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">C. Equally consider articles that both support and challenge my belief.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I4.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I4.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I4.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">D. Prioritize articles that challenge my belief but still consider supporting views.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I4.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I4.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I4.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">E. Only choose articles that challenge my belief, ignoring supporting or neutral viewpoints.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</blockquote>
<p id="A3.SS2.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS2.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">b) Scene-Adjustable Placeholders:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We constructed 15 unique <span id="A3.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Controversial Topic]</span> and article sets, covering a diverse range of issues, resulting in 15 prompt variations.</p>
<ul id="A3.I5" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A3.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">[Controversial Topic]</span>: The topic being evaluated (e.g., “Climate change is primarily caused by human activity.”; “The death penalty is an effective deterrent to crime.”).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">[Supports Belief]</span>: Article aligned with the presumed pre-existing view (e.g., “A study supporting the effectiveness of the death penalty.”).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I5.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">[Challenges Belief]</span>: Article contradicting the presumed view (e.g., “Research indicating no correlation between the death penalty and crime rates.”).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I5.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I5.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I5.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I5.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">[Neutral or Unrelated]</span>: Article unrelated to the topic or providing a neutral stance (e.g., “An analysis of global prison systems”).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Accordingly, by substituting the cognitive bias type (<math id="A3.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=</annotation></semantics></math> Confirmation Bias), the scenario type (Biased Information Seeking Paradigm, <math id="A3.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math>), and <math id="A3.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=15</annotation></semantics></math> into Equations (1) and (2), the <span id="A3.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Confirmation Bias Index under Biased Information Seeking Paradigm</span> is defined as:</p>
<table id="A3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CBI}_{\mathrm{Conf}}^{(k=2)}=\frac{1}{15}\sum_{i=1}^{15}\sum_{j=1}^{5}(5-j)\times P_{i}(O_{j})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>CBI</mi><mi>Conf</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>15</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>15</mn></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>5</mn></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CBI}_{\mathrm{Conf}}^{(k=2)}=\frac{1}{15}\sum_{i=1}^{15}\sum_{j=1}^{5}(5-j)\times P_{i}(O_{j})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3 </span>Framing Effect Testbed</h3>

<div id="A3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Framing effect refers to the phenomenon where individuals respond differently to identical information depending on whether it is presented as a potential gain or a potential loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tversky &amp; Kahneman (1981)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kahneman &amp; Tversky (2013)</a></cite>. This cognitive bias has been robustly demonstrated in classic experiments. For example, in the <span id="A3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Asian Disease</span> study, participants tended to make more risk-averse decisions when outcomes were positively framed (e.g., “200 will be saved”), but exhibited greater risk-seeking behavior when the same outcomes were presented as losses (e.g., “400 will die”) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tversky &amp; Kahneman (1981)</a></cite>. Similarly, in the <span id="A3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Investment/Insurance</span> study, individuals were more likely to purchase insurance when risks were described as potential losses rather than as missed gains, highlighting how framing can significantly influence decision-making <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kahneman &amp; Tversky (2013)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To systematically examine the framing effect in LLM-based agents, we adapt two classic paradigms: the <span id="A3.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Asian Disease Paradigm</span> (assessing under risk decision-making) and the <span id="A3.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Investment/Insurance Paradigm</span> (testing under everyday decision-making). Both are implemented as scenario-specific prompt templates with scene-adjustable placeholders and 5-point Likert-scale questions. By generating multiple prompt variations for each paradigm, we construct a rigorous <span id="A3.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Framing Effect Testbed</span> for quantitative assessment via the <span id="A3.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Framing Effect Index</span>.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A3.SS3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">1. Asian Disease Paradigm:<span id="A3.SS3.p2.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Testing Framing Effect via Risk Decision-Making</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate the impact of gain/loss framing on risk preferences, we adapt the <span id="A3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Asian Disease</span> study <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tversky &amp; Kahneman (1981)</a></cite>. Each prompt presents a hypothetical problem and two alternative programs, with outcomes identically described in either a positive (gain) or negative (loss) frame.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A3.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">a) Scenario Prompt Template:</span></p>
<blockquote id="A3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A3.SS3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_p">You are presented with a scenario where <span id="A3.SS3.p3.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Problem]</span>. Two programs are proposed to address the situation: 
<br class="ltx_break">Program A: <span id="A3.SS3.p3.2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Gain Frame]</span> 
<br class="ltx_break">Program B: <span id="A3.SS3.p3.2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Loss Frame]</span> 
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.0pt;">
<span id="A3.SS3.p3.2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Which of the following best reflects your choice?</span></p>
<ul id="A3.I6" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A3.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A. I strongly prefer the positively framed option (Program A).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">B. I somewhat prefer the positively framed option (Program A).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I6.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I6.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I6.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">C. I slightly prefer the positively framed option (Program A).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I6.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I6.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I6.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">D. I am somewhat undecided between the two options.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I6.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I6.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I6.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">E. I see both options as equivalent and have no preference.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</blockquote>
<p id="A3.SS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS3.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">b) Scene-Adjustable Placeholders:</span>
We constructed 15 unique <span id="A3.SS3.p3.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Problem]</span>, <span id="A3.SS3.p3.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Gain Frame]</span>, and <span id="A3.SS3.p3.3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Loss Frame]</span> sets based on classic and generalized framing scenarios, resulting in 15 prompt variations.</p>
<ul id="A3.I7" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A3.I7.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I7.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I7.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I7.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">[Problem]</span>: The scenario or problem being addressed (e.g., “A disease outbreak is expected to kill 600 people unless a program is implemented.”).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I7.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I7.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I7.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I7.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">[Gain Frame]</span>: The positively framed outcome (e.g., “Program A will save 200 people.”).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I7.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I7.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I7.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I7.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">[Loss Frame]</span>: The negatively framed outcome (e.g., “Program B will result in 400 people dying.”).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Accordingly, by substituting the cognitive bias type (<math id="A3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=</annotation></semantics></math> Framing Effect), the scenario type (Asian Disease Paradigm, <math id="A3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>), and <math id="A3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=15</annotation></semantics></math> into Equations (1) and (2), the <span id="A3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Framing Effect Index under Asian Disease Paradigm</span> is defined as:</p>
<table id="A3.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CBI}_{\mathrm{Framing}}^{(k=1)}=\frac{1}{15}\sum_{i=1}^{15}\sum_{j=1}^{5}(5-j)\times P_{i}(O_{j})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>CBI</mi><mi>Framing</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>15</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>15</mn></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>5</mn></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CBI}_{\mathrm{Framing}}^{(k=1)}=\frac{1}{15}\sum_{i=1}^{15}\sum_{j=1}^{5}(5-j)\times P_{i}(O_{j})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">2. Investment/Insurance Paradigm:<span id="A3.SS3.p4.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Testing Framing Effect via Everyday Decision Framing</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To assess the influence of positive and negative framing in everyday decision-making, we adapt the <span id="A3.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Investment/Insurance</span> study <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kahneman &amp; Tversky (2013)</a></cite>. Each scenario describes a decision context and presents two options, differing only in the framing of equivalent outcomes.
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="A3.SS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">a) Scenario Prompt Template:</span></p>
<blockquote id="A3.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A3.SS3.p5.2.1" class="ltx_p">You are considering <span id="A3.SS3.p5.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Everyday Decision]</span>. The following statements describe the same situation with different wording: 
<br class="ltx_break">Option A: <span id="A3.SS3.p5.2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Positive Frame]</span> 
<br class="ltx_break">Option B: <span id="A3.SS3.p5.2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Negative Frame]</span> 
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:5.0pt;">
<span id="A3.SS3.p5.2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Which option sounds more appealing or persuasive to you?</span></p>
<ul id="A3.I8" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A3.I8.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I8.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I8.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A. I strongly prefer the positively framed option (Option A).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I8.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I8.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I8.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">B. I somewhat prefer the positively framed option (Option A).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I8.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I8.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I8.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">C. I slightly prefer the positively framed option (Option A).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I8.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I8.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I8.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">D. I am somewhat undecided between the two options.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I8.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I8.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I8.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">E. I see both options as equivalent and have no preference.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</blockquote>
<p id="A3.SS3.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS3.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">b) Scene-Adjustable Placeholders:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We constructed 15 unique <span id="A3.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Everyday Decision]</span>, <span id="A3.SS3.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Positive Frame]</span>, and <span id="A3.SS3.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Negative Frame]</span> sets, resulting in 15 prompt variations.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="A3.I9" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A3.I9.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I9.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I9.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I9.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">[Everyday Decision]</span>: The decision-making context (e.g., “You are considering whether to buy insurance for your car.”).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I9.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I9.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.I9.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I9.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">[Positive Frame]</span>: The positively framed description (e.g., “Buying insurance ensures you are protected against potential losses.”).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A3.I9.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A3.I9.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.I9.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.I9.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">[Negative Frame]</span>: The negatively framed description (e.g., “Not buying insurance exposes you to significant financial risks.”).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Accordingly, by substituting the cognitive bias type (<math id="A3.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=</annotation></semantics></math> Framing Effect), the scenario type (Investment/Insurance Paradigm, <math id="A3.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math>), and <math id="A3.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=15</annotation></semantics></math> into Equations (1) and (2), the <span id="A3.SS3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Framing Effect Index under Investment/Insurance Paradigm</span> is defined as:</p>
<table id="A3.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{CBI}_{\mathrm{Framing}}^{(k=2)}=\frac{1}{15}\sum_{i=1}^{15}\sum_{j=1}^{5}(5-j)\times P_{i}(O_{j})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>CBI</mi><mi>Framing</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>15</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>15</mn></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>5</mn></munderover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{CBI}_{\mathrm{Framing}}^{(k=2)}=\frac{1}{15}\sum_{i=1}^{15}\sum_{j=1}^{5}(5-j)\times P_{i}(O_{j})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Behavioral Regulation Engine Implementation Details</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents the implementation details of the Behavioral Regulation Engine.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Prompt Numerical Control</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The numerical value <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\%</annotation></semantics></math> in the prompt template was linearly mapped from the normalized Control Coefficient <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. A coefficient of <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to an instruction of <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=\lambda\times 100\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=\lambda\times 100\%</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Representation Engineering (RepE) Control</h3>

<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dataset Generation</h5>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Generation Prompt:</span> The scenarios were generated by prompting state-of-the-art LLMs (as listed in Section 7.2). For example, for the Bandwagon Effect:</p>
<blockquote id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_p">Generate {num_scenarios} different bandwagon effect scenarios. Each scenario must be from a DIFFERENT domain with varied structure and syntax.</p>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_p">Each scenario should include specific social proof numbers, a decision point about following the crowd, and five concise multiple-choice options (A-E).</p>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.3" class="ltx_p">Based on the UnitTest format, use this structure and example:</p>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.4" class="ltx_p">**EXAMPLE:**
[An example from Classic Social Experiment Testbed]</p>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.5" class="ltx_p">For each scenario, please vary the structure and syntax - use different sentence patterns, lengths, and approaches. Number the scenario.</p>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.6" class="ltx_p">OUTPUT FORMAT:</p>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.7" class="ltx_p">1. [Scenario text here with A-E options]</p>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.8" class="ltx_p">2. [Scenario text here with A-E options]</p>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.9" class="ltx_p">3. [Scenario text here with A-E options]</p>
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.10" class="ltx_p">Generate {num_scenarios} scenarios now:</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Activation Extraction and Layer Selection</h5>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The hidden states <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> were extracted with the following specifics:</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Layer Selection:</span> Instead of pre-selecting a fixed layer, we adopted an empirical approach. We performed a layer-wise accuracy evaluation on a test set of our contrastive samples. For each bias, we identified the layers whose representations were most predictive of the bias direction (i.e., had the highest accuracy in classifying positive vs. negative pairs). Control interventions were then applied to the activations of the top 15 performing layers.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Control Coefficient</span>
To ensure model stability, we first determine the operational range for the control parameter, lambda (<math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>). Excessively large positive or negative <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> values can corrupt the model’s output. For instance, a model that initially assigns probabilities of [0.6, 0.1, 0.1, 0.1, 0.1] to valid choices ’A’, ’B’, ’C’, ’D’, ’E’ might, after an extreme intervention, produce a distribution like [0.01, 0.02, 0.01, 0.01, 0.01] for the same choices, with most of the probability shifting to irrelevant tokens like ”.” or ”¡eos¿”. Our method identifies the maximum and minimum <math id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> values that maintain a high probability for valid choices (e.g., over 90% of the initial probability), thus defining a stable operational range tailored to each model and task.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.3 </span>Fine-Tuning Control</h3>

<section id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LoRA and Training Hyperparameters</h5>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We used the PEFT library with the following LoRA configuration:</p>
<ul id="A4.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="A4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rank (r):</span> 8</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lora Alpha:</span> 16</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lora Dropout:</span> 0.05</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Target Modules:</span> (‘q_proj‘, ‘k_proj‘, ‘v_proj‘, ‘o_proj‘)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Learning Rate:</span> 2e-4</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Batch Size:</span> 4</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Max Steps:</span> 200</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I1.i8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Warmup Ratio:</span> 0.05</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i9" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I1.i9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gradient Accumulation Steps:</span> 4</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
